LS_REG('part',6,{"toc":[[2,"h2","2.1 Sources of history and the ancient period (इतिहासका स्रोत र प्राचीन काल)"],[2,"h13","2.2 Lichhavi period (लिच्छविकाल)"],[2,"h24","2.3 Medieval period (मध्यकाल)"],[2,"h35","2.4 Unification to the Sugauli Treaty (एकीकरणदेखि सुगौली सन्धिसम्म)"],[2,"h46","2.5 Rana period (राणाकाल), 1846–1951"],[2,"h57","2.6 The revolution of 2007 BS and the decade to 2017 BS (२००७ सालको क्रान्ति र त्यसपछिको दशक)"],[2,"h68","2.7 Panchayat period (पञ्चायतकाल), 2017–2046 BS"],[2,"h79","2.8 From the People's Movement of 2046 to the peace of 2063 (२०४६ देखि २०६३ सम्म)"],[2,"h90","2.9 Republic, Constitution and events to the present (गणतन्त्र, संविधान र हालसम्मका घटना)"],[2,"h101","2.10 Timeline: treaties, wars, constitutions and firsts (सन्धि, युद्ध, संविधान र पहिला घटना)"],[2,"h112","Chapter 2 — End matter"],[3,"h113","A. Quick Revision"],[3,"h114","B. Top 20 facts"],[3,"h115","C. Top 25 MCQs"],[3,"h116","D. Five short-answer questions"],[3,"h117","E. Common mistakes"],[3,"h118","F. Status of the facts in this chapter"],[3,"h119","G. References"]],"html":"<h1 id=\"h1\">PART 6 — CHAPTER 2: History of Nepal (नेपालको इतिहास)<\/h1>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Book-plan section<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>B<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Priority<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>High<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Official units<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Madhesh, Gandaki, Lumbini, Sudurpashchim: unit 1.5 — <strong>2 questions<\/strong> (the Madhesh unit title is inferred). Koshi: unit 1.2 — 4 questions, shared with world history. Bagmati: unit 1.2 — 3 questions (as read). Karnali: examined; unit number and count not legible.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Province-specific wording<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Karnali names the Khas kingdom, the Sinja civilisation, the Shah dynasty and events since 2007 BS. Lumbini names the Malla period. Koshi: ancient period to the present.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Facts checked on<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>11 November 2026 (25 Kartik 2083 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Questions in this chapter<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>75 MCQs (50 in topics + 25 at the end) and 15 short-answer questions<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>How to read the marks<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>🔄 <strong>UPDATE<\/strong> — a recent fact that may change. Check it again before your examination.<\/li>\n<li>⚖️ <strong>DISPUTED<\/strong> — published sources disagree. Learn both versions.<\/li>\n<li><code>[Pattern-Based – Reconstructed]<\/code> — written to an official question format; not a real past question.<\/li>\n<li><code>[Expected – Practice]<\/code> — an original practice question on a point the syllabus names.<\/li>\n<li>No question in this chapter is a verified past question.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>A note on dates.<\/strong> BS means Bikram Sambat; AD means the Common Era. Every BS–AD pair after 1975 BS was worked out with a calendar-conversion tool. For earlier events the year is normally given, and a full date only where the textbooks agree on it.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Three periods of Nepal's history<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Period<\/th>\n<th>From<\/th>\n<th>To<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ancient (प्राचीन काल)<\/td>\n<td>Earliest times<\/td>\n<td>879 AD — the start of Nepal Sambat<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Medieval (मध्यकाल)<\/td>\n<td>879 AD<\/td>\n<td>1768 AD — the conquest of Kathmandu by Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Modern (आधुनिक काल)<\/td>\n<td>1768 AD<\/td>\n<td>Today<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Topics<\/strong><\/p>\n<p>2.1 Sources of history and the ancient period · 2.2 Lichhavi period · 2.3 Medieval period · 2.4 Unification to the Sugauli Treaty · 2.5 Rana period · 2.6 Revolution of 2007 BS and the decade to 2017 BS · 2.7 Panchayat period · 2.8 From 2046 BS to 2063 BS · 2.9 Republic, Constitution and events to the present · 2.10 Timeline: treaties, wars, constitutions and firsts<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h2\">2.1 Sources of history and the ancient period (इतिहासका स्रोत र प्राचीन काल)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h3\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Questions on the oldest dynasties, the first inscriptions and the life of the Buddha are short and factual. They also link with the culture unit (Lumbini, Janakpur) and the province chapters.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h4\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>We know the past from <strong>sources<\/strong>: things written on stone or metal, coins, old buildings, old books and the reports of foreign visitors. For the earliest period of the Kathmandu Valley there are no dated records. Its story comes from chronicles (वंशावली) written many centuries later, so it is part history and part tradition. Dated history begins with the Lichhavi inscriptions.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h5\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Sources<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Inscriptions (अभिलेख)<\/strong> on stone and copper are the most reliable source, because they were made at the time of the event.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>oldest inscription found in Nepal<\/strong> is the <strong>Ashoka pillar at Lumbini (249 BC)<\/strong>. It is in the <strong>Brahmi<\/strong> script and the Prakrit language. It says that the Buddha was born there and that the village's tax was reduced.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>oldest dated inscription of the Kathmandu Valley<\/strong> is the pillar of King <strong>Manadeva at Changu Narayan (464 AD)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Coins:<\/strong> the <strong>Mananka<\/strong> of Manadeva is regarded as the first coin of Nepal.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Chronicles:<\/strong> the <strong>Gopal Raj Vamshavali<\/strong>, written in the time of Jayasthiti Malla, is the oldest surviving chronicle.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Foreign accounts:<\/strong> Kautilya's <em>Arthashastra<\/em> mentions the woollen blankets of Nepal. The Allahabad pillar inscription of Samudragupta names Nepal as a frontier state. The Chinese pilgrims <strong>Faxian<\/strong> (5th century) and <strong>Xuanzang<\/strong> (7th century) described Lumbini and Kapilvastu.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Tradition about the Kathmandu Valley<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Valley is said to have been a lake. In Buddhist tradition <strong>Manjushri<\/strong> cut the hill at <strong>Chobhar<\/strong> and drained it.<\/li>\n<li>The name &quot;Nepal&quot; is linked by tradition with a sage called <strong>Ne Muni<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The early dynasties of the Valley<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Gopal dynasty<\/strong> — cow-herders; the first rulers in the chronicles; 8 kings; first king <strong>Bhuktaman<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mahispal dynasty<\/strong> — buffalo-herders; 3 kings.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kirat dynasty<\/strong> — first king <strong>Yalambar<\/strong>, last king <strong>Gasti<\/strong>. Most chronicles count <strong>29 kings<\/strong>; the Gopal Raj Vamshavali counts 32 ⚖️.<\/li>\n<li>Tradition says <strong>Gautam Buddha<\/strong> visited the Valley in the time of the 7th Kirat king, <strong>Jitedasti<\/strong>, and Emperor <strong>Ashoka<\/strong> in the time of the 14th, <strong>Sthunko<\/strong>. Neither visit is proved by a record.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Soma dynasty<\/strong> — 5 kings, from Nimisha to Bhaskar Varma. The Lichhavis followed.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Outside the Valley<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Videha (Mithila)<\/strong> — capital <strong>Janakpur<\/strong>; King Janak; birthplace of Sita.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Shakya state of Kapilvastu<\/strong> — capital at <strong>Tilaurakot<\/strong>; King Suddhodhana, father of the Buddha.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Koliya state<\/strong> — Devdaha and Ramgram; the family of the Buddha's mother, Mayadevi.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Gautam Buddha<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Born at <strong>Lumbini<\/strong> as Siddhartha Gautam, son of Suddhodhana and Mayadevi.<\/li>\n<li>⚖️ <strong>Year of birth:<\/strong> most textbooks give <strong>563 BC<\/strong>. The World Heritage listing of Lumbini gives <strong>623 BC<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Enlightenment at Bodh Gaya; first sermon at Sarnath; death (Mahaparinirvana) at Kushinagar at the age of 80.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ashoka<\/strong> visited Lumbini in <strong>249 BC<\/strong>, the 20th year of his reign, and set up the pillar.<\/li>\n<li>The Lumbini pillar was rediscovered in <strong>1896<\/strong> by <strong>Khadga Shamsher<\/strong> and the German archaeologist <strong>Dr Alois Führer<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Lumbini was listed as a <strong>World Heritage Site in 1997<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h6\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Early dynasties of the Kathmandu Valley (from the chronicles)<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Order<\/th>\n<th>Dynasty<\/th>\n<th>Kings<\/th>\n<th>First king<\/th>\n<th>Remembered for<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>Gopal<\/td>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>Bhuktaman<\/td>\n<td>Cow-herders; first rulers<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>Mahispal<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>Bar Singh<\/td>\n<td>Buffalo-herders<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>Kirat<\/td>\n<td>29 (or 32)<\/td>\n<td>Yalambar<\/td>\n<td>Long rule; last king Gasti<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>Soma<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>Nimisha<\/td>\n<td>Last king Bhaskar Varma<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>Lichhavi<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>See topic 2.2<\/td>\n<td>Dated history begins<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Kinds of sources<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kind<\/th>\n<th>Examples<\/th>\n<th>Value<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Inscriptions<\/td>\n<td>Lumbini pillar; Changu Narayan pillar<\/td>\n<td>Most reliable; written at the time<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Coins<\/td>\n<td>Mananka<\/td>\n<td>Show rulers, religion and trade<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Monuments and excavations<\/td>\n<td>Tilaurakot, Lumbini, Changu Narayan<\/td>\n<td>Show settlement, art and belief<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chronicles<\/td>\n<td>Gopal Raj Vamshavali<\/td>\n<td>Give lists of kings; written later, so less reliable<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Foreign accounts<\/td>\n<td>Faxian, Xuanzang, <em>Arthashastra<\/em><\/td>\n<td>Outside view; help to fix dates<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h7\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Ashoka at Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>249 BC<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Changu Narayan inscription<\/td>\n<td>464 AD (Shaka Sambat 386)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Birth of the Buddha<\/td>\n<td>563 BC (textbooks) or 623 BC (World Heritage listing) ⚖️<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rediscovery of the Lumbini pillar<\/td>\n<td>1896<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini on the World Heritage List<\/td>\n<td>1997<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kings: Gopal / Mahispal / Kirat / Soma<\/td>\n<td>8 / 3 / 29 or 32 / 5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h8\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Order of dynasties: &quot;Go – Ma – Ki – So – Li&quot;<\/strong> — Gopal, Mahispal, Kirat, Soma, Lichhavi.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;7 for Buddha, 14 for Ashoka&quot;:<\/strong> the 7th Kirat king (Jitedasti) and the 14th (Sthunko). Fourteen is double seven.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Yalambar begins, Gasti ends.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Life of the Buddha in four places: L – B – S – K<\/strong> — Lumbini (birth), Bodh Gaya (enlightenment), Sarnath (first sermon), Kushinagar (death).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h9\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;Oldest inscription in Nepal&quot;<\/strong> = Ashoka pillar, Lumbini. <strong>&quot;Oldest dated inscription of the Kathmandu Valley&quot;<\/strong> = Changu Narayan. Read the wording.<\/li>\n<li>The visits of the Buddha and Ashoka to the Kathmandu Valley are <strong>tradition<\/strong>, not recorded history.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Lumbini is in Rupandehi<\/strong>; <strong>Tilaurakot is in Kapilvastu<\/strong>; <strong>Ramgram is in Nawalparasi West<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The number of Kirat kings is given as 29 or 32 ⚖️.<\/li>\n<li>The Buddha's birth year has two versions ⚖️. If only one of them is among the options, choose it.<\/li>\n<li>Lumbini joined the World Heritage List in <strong>1997<\/strong>, not 1979. The year 1979 belongs to the Kathmandu Valley and Sagarmatha National Park.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h10\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Ancient-period points<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Homeland of the Kirat peoples (Limbuwan and Khambuwan in later history)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>Videha or Mithila; Janakpur, capital of King Janak and birthplace of Sita<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Gopal, Mahispal, Kirat and Soma dynasties of the Kathmandu Valley; Chobhar; Changu Narayan<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Kali Gandaki valley, known for sacred <em>shaligram<\/em> stones and old pilgrim routes to Muktinath<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Lumbini, Tilaurakot (Kapilvastu), Devdaha and Ramgram; the Ashoka pillars of Lumbini and Niglihawa<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>Early home of the Khas people, whose kingdom rose in the medieval period (topic 2.3)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Old hill settlements of Doti, which became a kingdom in the medieval period<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h11\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.1.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who is named as the first king of the Kirat dynasty?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Gasti<\/li>\n<li>(b) Yalambar<\/li>\n<li>(c) Jitedasti<\/li>\n<li>(d) Sthunko<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Yalambar was the first Kirat king and Gasti the last.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.1.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the dynasties in the order in which they ruled the Kathmandu Valley.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Kirat<\/li>\n<li>Gopal<\/li>\n<li>Lichhavi<\/li>\n<li>Mahispal<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2 – 1 – 4 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(b) 4 – 2 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2 – 4 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Gopal, Mahispal, Kirat, then (after the short Soma dynasty) Lichhavi.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.1.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match List I with List II.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>List I<\/th>\n<th>List II<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Changu Narayan pillar<\/td>\n<td>1. Coin<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Lumbini pillar<\/td>\n<td>2. Chronicle<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Gopal Raj Vamshavali<\/td>\n<td>3. Inscription of Manadeva<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Mananka<\/td>\n<td>4. Inscription of Ashoka<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-2, B-4, C-3, D-1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — The Changu Narayan pillar carries Manadeva's inscription, the Lumbini pillar Ashoka's; the Gopal Raj Vamshavali is a chronicle and the Mananka a coin.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.1.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Ashoka set up the pillar at Lumbini in 249 BC.<\/li>\n<li>The Lumbini inscription is written in the Brahmi script.<\/li>\n<li>Lumbini was placed on the World Heritage List in 1979.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Lumbini was listed in 1997.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.1.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>According to tradition, Gautam Buddha visited the Kathmandu Valley during the reign of which Kirat king?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Yalambar<\/li>\n<li>(b) Sthunko<\/li>\n<li>(c) Gasti<\/li>\n<li>(d) Jitedasti<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Jitedasti was the 7th Kirat king. Tradition places Ashoka's visit in the time of Sthunko, the 14th.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h12\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> What are the main sources of the history of ancient Nepal? Why are inscriptions considered the most reliable?<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> The sources are of two kinds. <strong>Archaeological sources<\/strong> are inscriptions on stone and copper, coins, monuments and the finds of excavations at places such as Lumbini and Tilaurakot. <strong>Literary sources<\/strong> are chronicles such as the Gopal Raj Vamshavali, religious texts, and the accounts of foreigners such as Faxian, Xuanzang and Kautilya. <strong>Inscriptions are the most reliable<\/strong> because (1) they were written at the time of the event, not centuries later; (2) they usually carry a date and the name of the ruler; (3) they are fixed on stone or metal and cannot easily be changed; (4) they record real acts such as grants, taxes and victories. Chronicles mix history with legend, so they are used only where inscriptions and coins support them.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h13\">2.2 Lichhavi period (लिच्छविकाल)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h14\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>This is the first period with dated records, and it is called the golden age of Nepal's history. Rulers, their palaces, coins and titles are favourite matching questions.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h15\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>The <strong>Lichhavis<\/strong> came to the Kathmandu Valley from <strong>Vaishali<\/strong>, in what is now Bihar. They ruled from about the 4th century AD to the 9th century. Under them Nepal had an organised government, coins, trade with Tibet and India, fine stone sculpture, and peace among Hindus and Buddhists. Their inscriptions are in the <strong>Sanskrit<\/strong> language.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h16\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Manadeva<\/strong> (ruled about 464–505 AD) is the first Lichhavi king proved by an inscription.\n<ul>\n<li>Inscription: <strong>Changu Narayan pillar, 464 AD<\/strong>. It praises his victories and his mother, Rajyavati.<\/li>\n<li>Coin: <strong>Mananka<\/strong>. Palace: <strong>Managriha<\/strong>. Monastery: <strong>Mana Vihar<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Amshuverma<\/strong> (about 605–621 AD) was not a Lichhavi by family. He rose as a high noble (<em>Mahasamanta<\/em>) under King Shivadeva I and then ruled in his own name.\n<ul>\n<li>Palace: <strong>Kailashkut Bhawan<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Book: <em><strong>Shabda Vidya<\/strong><\/em>, a work on grammar.<\/li>\n<li>The Chinese pilgrim Xuanzang praised his learning.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Bhrikuti<\/strong>, who married the Tibetan king <strong>Srong-tsen Gampo<\/strong> and helped to spread Buddhism in Tibet, is traditionally described as his daughter ⚖️. Some historians call her the daughter of King Udayadeva. In Tibet she is honoured as the Green Tara.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li>After Amshuverma, the <strong>Abhira Guptas<\/strong> (Jishnugupta, Vishnugupta) held real power while a Lichhavi king kept the throne. This is called <strong>dual rule<\/strong> (द्वैध शासन).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Narendradeva<\/strong> won back full Lichhavi rule with Tibetan help. Palace: <strong>Bhadradhivas<\/strong>. In his time the road between Tibet and India through Nepal became busy, and Chinese envoys visited the court.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Jayadeva II<\/strong> set up the Pashupati inscription (733 AD), which lists the Lichhavi kings.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Nepal Sambat<\/strong> began in <strong>879 AD<\/strong>, in the reign of <strong>Raghavadeva<\/strong>. Tradition links it with the merchant <strong>Shankhadhar Sakhwa<\/strong>, who is said to have paid off the debts of the people. This date is used to mark the end of the ancient period.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Government and society<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Local councils were called <strong>Panchali<\/strong>. Groups that looked after temples, water and festivals were called <strong>Gosthi<\/strong> — the root of today's <em>guthi<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Three taxes:<\/strong> <em>bhaga<\/em> (on farm produce), <em>bhoga<\/em> (on animals), <em>kara<\/em> (on trade).<\/li>\n<li>Unpaid compulsory labour was called <em>vishti<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li>Shaivism, Vaishnavism and Buddhism lived side by side.<\/li>\n<li>Nepal traded with Tibet and India in wool, musk, herbs and metal goods.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Art<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Changu Narayan<\/strong> is regarded as the oldest temple of the Valley.<\/li>\n<li>Famous stone images: <strong>Vishnu Vikranta<\/strong> (467 AD), the reclining Vishnu of <strong>Budhanilkantha<\/strong>, and <strong>Palanchok Bhagawati<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h17\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Lichhavi rulers to remember<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ruler<\/th>\n<th>About (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Palace<\/th>\n<th>Remembered for<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Manadeva<\/td>\n<td>464–505<\/td>\n<td>Managriha<\/td>\n<td>Changu Narayan inscription; Mananka coin; Mana Vihar<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shivadeva I<\/td>\n<td>Late 6th century<\/td>\n<td>Managriha<\/td>\n<td>Raised Amshuverma to power<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amshuverma<\/td>\n<td>605–621<\/td>\n<td>Kailashkut Bhawan<\/td>\n<td><em>Shabda Vidya<\/em>; praised by Xuanzang; link with Bhrikuti<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Narendradeva<\/td>\n<td>7th century<\/td>\n<td>Bhadradhivas<\/td>\n<td>Restored Lichhavi rule; ties with Tibet and China<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jayadeva II<\/td>\n<td>Early 8th century<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>Pashupati inscription (733 AD)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raghavadeva<\/td>\n<td>879<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>Nepal Sambat begins<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Lichhavi terms<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Term<\/th>\n<th>Meaning<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Panchali<\/td>\n<td>Local council<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gosthi<\/td>\n<td>Group managing religious and public works<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bhaga<\/td>\n<td>Tax on farm produce<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bhoga<\/td>\n<td>Tax on animals<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kara<\/td>\n<td>Tax on trade<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vishti<\/td>\n<td>Unpaid compulsory labour<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mahasamanta<\/td>\n<td>High noble; title of Amshuverma before he ruled<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h18\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Changu Narayan inscription<\/td>\n<td>464 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vishnu Vikranta image<\/td>\n<td>467 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amshuverma<\/td>\n<td>about 605–621 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pashupati inscription of Jayadeva II<\/td>\n<td>733 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepal Sambat begins<\/td>\n<td>879 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepal Sambat to AD<\/td>\n<td>add 879 or 880<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h19\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;Mana × 3&quot;:<\/strong> <strong>Mana<\/strong>deva — <strong>Mana<\/strong>griha, <strong>Mana<\/strong>nka, <strong>Mana<\/strong> Vihar.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Three palaces in order: M – K – B<\/strong> — Managriha (Manadeva), Kailashkut (Amshuverma), Bhadradhivas (Narendradeva).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Three taxes: &quot;Bhaga for the field, Bhoga for the herd, Kara for the trader.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Three eras:<\/strong> Bikram Sambat = AD + 57; Shaka Sambat = AD − 78; Nepal Sambat = AD − 879.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h20\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Amshuverma was not a Lichhavi<\/strong> by birth, although he is the best-known ruler of the period.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Changu Narayan inscription is Manadeva's<\/strong>, not Amshuverma's.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Araniko belongs to the Malla period<\/strong>, not the Lichhavi period.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Bhrikuti's father<\/strong> is given as Amshuverma in most textbooks and as Udayadeva by some historians ⚖️.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Nepal Sambat began under Raghavadeva<\/strong>; Shankhadhar Sakhwa is the merchant linked with it, not a king.<\/li>\n<li>&quot;Golden age&quot; (स्वर्णयुग) refers to the <strong>Lichhavi period<\/strong>. Do not give it to the Malla period, which is known for art and architecture.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h21\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Lichhavi-period points<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Manadeva's inscription speaks of lords of the east who accepted his rule; the eastern hills remained the home of Kirat peoples<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>The Lichhavis came from Vaishali, across the present border from Madhesh; Mithila kept its own tradition of learning<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Centre of Lichhavi rule: Changu Narayan (Bhaktapur), Hadigaun, Pashupati, Budhanilkantha, Palanchok (Kavrepalanchok)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Manadeva's inscription records a campaign across the Gandaki river against a rival power in the west<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Lumbini and Kapilvastu were visited and described by Faxian and Xuanzang<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>Lies outside the area of known Lichhavi records; the Khas kingdom rose here later<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Lies outside the area of known Lichhavi records<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h22\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.2.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which Lichhavi king issued the coin called Mananka?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Manadeva<\/li>\n<li>(b) Amshuverma<\/li>\n<li>(c) Narendradeva<\/li>\n<li>(d) Shivadeva I<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — The Mananka of Manadeva is regarded as the first coin of Nepal.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.2.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the palace with the ruler who is linked with it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Palace<\/th>\n<th>Ruler<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Managriha<\/td>\n<td>1. Narendradeva<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Kailashkut Bhawan<\/td>\n<td>2. Manadeva<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Bhadradhivas<\/td>\n<td>3. Amshuverma<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-2, B-1, C-3<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-3, B-2, C-1<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-2, B-3, C-1<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-1, B-3, C-2<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Managriha–Manadeva, Kailashkut Bhawan–Amshuverma, Bhadradhivas–Narendradeva.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.2.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which one does not belong to the group?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Managriha<\/li>\n<li>(b) Kailashkut Bhawan<\/li>\n<li>(c) Bhadradhivas<\/li>\n<li>(d) Hanuman Dhoka<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — The first three are Lichhavi palaces. Hanuman Dhoka is the Malla and Shah palace of Kathmandu.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.2.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The Lichhavis came to Nepal from Vaishali.<\/li>\n<li>Amshuverma wrote <em>Shabda Vidya<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li>The Changu Narayan pillar inscription was set up by Amshuverma.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — The Changu Narayan inscription (464 AD) was set up by Manadeva.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.2.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In the Lichhavi period, the tax on farm produce was called:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Bhoga<\/li>\n<li>(b) Bhaga<\/li>\n<li>(c) Kara<\/li>\n<li>(d) Vishti<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Bhaga was the tax on farm produce, bhoga on animals and kara on trade. Vishti was unpaid labour.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h23\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> Why is the Lichhavi period called the golden age of Nepal's history?<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> The Lichhavi period is called the golden age for five reasons. <strong>(1) Government:<\/strong> there was an organised administration with local councils (Panchali), fixed taxes (bhaga, bhoga, kara) and written royal orders. <strong>(2) Economy:<\/strong> coins such as the Mananka were issued, and trade with Tibet and India made the country rich. <strong>(3) Art:<\/strong> stone sculpture reached a very high level, as in the Vishnu Vikranta image and the Budhanilkantha Vishnu; Changu Narayan temple dates from this time. <strong>(4) Learning:<\/strong> Sanskrit was used for inscriptions, and rulers such as Amshuverma were scholars. <strong>(5) Religious harmony and foreign relations:<\/strong> Hindus and Buddhists lived in peace, and Nepal built friendly ties with Tibet and China, shown by the marriage of Bhrikuti. Because the country made progress in every field at once, historians call it a golden age.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h24\">2.3 Medieval period (मध्यकाल)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h25\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>The Malla kings left the temples, palaces and festivals that are still Nepal's best-known heritage. The Karnali syllabus names the <strong>Khas kingdom<\/strong> and the <strong>Sinja civilisation<\/strong>; the Lumbini syllabus names the <strong>Malla period<\/strong>. The Karnat dynasty of <strong>Simraungadh<\/strong> matters for Madhesh.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h26\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>In the medieval period Nepal was not one country. The <strong>Malla<\/strong> kings ruled the Kathmandu Valley, which split into three kingdoms after 1482. In the west the <strong>Khas<\/strong> kingdom of Sinja was large and strong, and later broke into the <strong>Baise<\/strong> (twenty-two) states. The <strong>Chaubise<\/strong> (twenty-four) states lay in the Gandaki region. In the southern plain the <strong>Karnat<\/strong> dynasty ruled from Simraungadh, and later the <strong>Sen<\/strong> kings held Palpa, Makwanpur and the east. One of the Chaubise states, <strong>Gorkha<\/strong>, would unite them all.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h27\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>The Malla kings of the Kathmandu Valley<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ari Malla<\/strong> (about 1200 AD) was the first Malla king.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Araniko<\/strong> (1245–1306) led a team of Nepali artists to Tibet and then to the court of <strong>Kublai Khan<\/strong> in China. He built the <strong>White Stupa in Beijing<\/strong>. He is a national hero (राष्ट्रिय विभूति).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Shamsuddin Ilyas<\/strong>, Sultan of Bengal, raided the Valley in <strong>1349<\/strong> and damaged Pashupati and Swayambhu.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Jayasthiti Malla<\/strong> (ruled 1382–1395) was the great reformer:\n<ul>\n<li>divided society into <strong>four classes and 64 castes<\/strong> by occupation;<\/li>\n<li>fixed <strong>weights and measures<\/strong> (<em>mana<\/em>, <em>pathi<\/em>);<\/li>\n<li>graded <strong>land into four classes<\/strong> (<em>abal, doyam, sim, chahar<\/em>);<\/li>\n<li>made written rules on houses, dress and crime.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Yaksha Malla<\/strong> (ruled 1428–1482) stretched the kingdom to its widest limits. After his death in <strong>1482<\/strong> it broke into <strong>Kantipur<\/strong> (Kathmandu), <strong>Lalitpur<\/strong> (Patan) and <strong>Bhaktapur<\/strong> (Bhadgaon).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kantipur:<\/strong> Ratna Malla (first king of separate Kantipur) · <strong>Mahendra Malla<\/strong> (Taleju temple, 1564; the silver coin <em>Mahendramalli<\/em>) · <strong>Pratap Malla<\/strong> (ruled 1641–1674; poet with the title <em>Kavindra<\/em>; <strong>Rani Pokhari<\/strong>, 1670; the Hanuman image at the palace gate, which gave the name Hanuman Dhoka) · <strong>Jaya Prakash Malla<\/strong> (last king; the Kumari House, 1757).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Lalitpur:<\/strong> <strong>Siddhi Narsingh Malla<\/strong> (the stone <strong>Krishna Mandir<\/strong>, 1637) · Yog Narendra Malla · <strong>Tej Narsingh Malla<\/strong> (last king).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Bhaktapur:<\/strong> <strong>Bhupatindra Malla<\/strong> (the five-storey <strong>Nyatapola<\/strong> temple, 1702; the Palace of 55 Windows) · <strong>Ranajit Malla<\/strong> (last king; the Golden Gate).<\/li>\n<li>The Malla period is known for the pagoda style, wood-carving, metalwork, festivals (<em>jatra<\/em>) and literature in Newar (Nepal Bhasa) and Maithili.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The Khas kingdom and the Sinja civilisation<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Founded by <strong>Nagaraj<\/strong> in about the 12th century. Capital: <strong>Sinja<\/strong>, in today's <strong>Jumla<\/strong> district. Winter capital: <strong>Dullu<\/strong>, in <strong>Dailekh<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>At its height it stretched over western Nepal, Kumaon and Garhwal, and parts of western Tibet.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Krachalla<\/strong> conquered Kumaon (inscription of 1223 AD).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Jitari Malla<\/strong> attacked the Kathmandu Valley in the 1280s.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ripu Malla<\/strong> visited Lumbini in <strong>1312<\/strong>; his name is cut on the Ashoka pillar.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Prithvi Malla<\/strong> set up the <strong>Dullu pillar inscription (1357 AD)<\/strong>, the main record of the dynasty.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Sinja valley is regarded as the birthplace of the Nepali (Khas) language<\/strong>. The oldest inscriptions in early Nepali come from the Dullu and Sinja area.<\/li>\n<li>After the late 14th century the kingdom broke into the <strong>Baise states<\/strong>. <strong>Jumla<\/strong> was the strongest.<\/li>\n<li>Sinja is on Nepal's tentative list for World Heritage.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The Karnat dynasty of Simraungadh<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Founded by <strong>Nanyadeva<\/strong> in <strong>1097 AD<\/strong>. Capital: <strong>Simraungadh<\/strong>, in today's <strong>Bara<\/strong> district. The kingdom is also called Tirhut or Mithila.<\/li>\n<li>The last king, <strong>Harisimhadeva<\/strong>, fled to the hills in <strong>1324<\/strong> when <strong>Ghiyasuddin Tughlaq<\/strong> of Delhi attacked. Tradition says his family carried the goddess <strong>Taleju Bhawani<\/strong> to Bhaktapur.<\/li>\n<li>The dynasty is remembered for Maithili learning, music and law.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Sen, Baise, Chaubise and Gorkha<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Mukunda Sen<\/strong> (16th century) made <strong>Palpa<\/strong> a large kingdom. It later divided; Sen kings ruled <strong>Makwanpur<\/strong>, <strong>Chaudandi<\/strong> and <strong>Bijayapur<\/strong> in the east.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Baise states<\/strong> — about 22 states in the Karnali region. <strong>Doti<\/strong> was a separate kingdom in the far west.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Chaubise states<\/strong> — about 24 states in the Gandaki region, among them Palpa, Tanahun, Lamjung, Kaski, Parbat and Gorkha.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Gorkha:<\/strong> <strong>Drabya Shah<\/strong>, a prince of Lamjung, founded the Shah kingdom of Gorkha in <strong>1559 AD (1616 BS)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ram Shah<\/strong> of Gorkha (17th century) fixed weights and measures and was famous for justice: <strong>&quot;न्याय नपाए गोर्खा जानू&quot;<\/strong> — &quot;If you do not get justice, go to Gorkha.&quot;<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h28\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Malla rulers and their works<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Ruler<\/th>\n<th>Kingdom<\/th>\n<th>Remembered for<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Jayasthiti Malla<\/td>\n<td>Undivided Valley<\/td>\n<td>Social and legal reforms; weights and measures<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Yaksha Malla<\/td>\n<td>Undivided Valley<\/td>\n<td>Largest extent; kingdom divided after 1482<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mahendra Malla<\/td>\n<td>Kantipur<\/td>\n<td>Taleju temple; silver coin<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pratap Malla<\/td>\n<td>Kantipur<\/td>\n<td>Rani Pokhari; Hanuman Dhoka; poet<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jaya Prakash Malla<\/td>\n<td>Kantipur<\/td>\n<td>Last king; Kumari House<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Siddhi Narsingh Malla<\/td>\n<td>Lalitpur<\/td>\n<td>Krishna Mandir<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tej Narsingh Malla<\/td>\n<td>Lalitpur<\/td>\n<td>Last king<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bhupatindra Malla<\/td>\n<td>Bhaktapur<\/td>\n<td>Nyatapola; Palace of 55 Windows<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ranajit Malla<\/td>\n<td>Bhaktapur<\/td>\n<td>Last king; Golden Gate<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Kingdoms outside the Valley<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kingdom<\/th>\n<th>Region today<\/th>\n<th>Capital<\/th>\n<th>Key names<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Khas<\/td>\n<td>Karnali; reached Kumaon and western Tibet<\/td>\n<td>Sinja (Jumla); winter capital Dullu (Dailekh)<\/td>\n<td>Nagaraj, Krachalla, Jitari Malla, Ripu Malla, Prithvi Malla<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnat (Tirhut)<\/td>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>Simraungadh (Bara)<\/td>\n<td>Nanyadeva, Harisimhadeva<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sen<\/td>\n<td>Lumbini, Bagmati, Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Palpa; later Makwanpur, Chaudandi, Bijayapur<\/td>\n<td>Mukunda Sen<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Baise<\/td>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>Jumla the strongest<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Chaubise<\/td>\n<td>Gandaki and Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>Palpa, Tanahun, Lamjung, Kaski, Gorkha<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gorkha<\/td>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Gorkha<\/td>\n<td>Drabya Shah, Ram Shah<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h29\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnat dynasty founded<\/td>\n<td>1097 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Araniko<\/td>\n<td>1245–1306<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ripu Malla at Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>1312<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Fall of Simraungadh<\/td>\n<td>1324<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Raid of Shamsuddin Ilyas<\/td>\n<td>1349<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dullu inscription of Prithvi Malla<\/td>\n<td>1357<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jayasthiti Malla<\/td>\n<td>1382–1395<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Death of Yaksha Malla; division<\/td>\n<td>1482<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Drabya Shah in Gorkha<\/td>\n<td>1559 AD (1616 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Krishna Mandir<\/td>\n<td>1637<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rani Pokhari<\/td>\n<td>1670<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nyatapola<\/td>\n<td>1702<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h30\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Last kings: &quot;J – T – R&quot;<\/strong> — <strong>J<\/strong>aya Prakash (Kantipur), <strong>T<\/strong>ej Narsingh (Lalitpur), <strong>R<\/strong>anajit (Bhaktapur).<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Jayasthiti sets the rules; Yaksha spreads the kingdom; his sons split it.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Khas kingdom: &quot;Sinja for summer, Dullu for winter.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;22 in Karnali, 24 in Gandaki&quot;:<\/strong> Baise in the west, Chaubise in the centre.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Gorkha: &quot;Drabya founds (1559), Ram gives justice, Prithvi Narayan unites.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h31\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Two &quot;Malla&quot; lines.<\/strong> The Malla kings of the Kathmandu Valley and the Khas kings of Sinja who used the name Malla (Jitari Malla, Ripu Malla, Prithvi Malla) are different dynasties.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dullu was the winter capital<\/strong>; Sinja was the main capital.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Simraungadh is in Bara<\/strong>, not in Dhanusha.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Krishna Mandir is in Patan<\/strong>; <strong>Nyatapola is in Bhaktapur<\/strong>; <strong>Rani Pokhari is in Kathmandu<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Jayasthiti Malla<\/strong> made the social reforms; <strong>Ram Shah<\/strong> made reforms in Gorkha. Both fixed weights and measures, so read the question.<\/li>\n<li>Araniko went to China in the <strong>Malla<\/strong> period.<\/li>\n<li>The numbers 22 and 24 are traditional; lists of the Baise and Chaubise states differ.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h32\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Medieval-period points<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Sen kingdoms of Chaudandi (Udayapur) and Bijayapur (near Dharan); Kirat chiefs of the eastern hills<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td><strong>Karnat dynasty at Simraungadh (Bara)<\/strong>, 1097–1324; Maithili language and learning; the plain later came under the Sen kings of Makwanpur<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Malla kingdoms of Kantipur, Lalitpur and Bhaktapur; Sen kingdom of Makwanpur<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Chaubise states; <strong>Gorkha<\/strong> of Drabya Shah and Ram Shah; Lamjung, Kaski, Tanahun<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td><strong>Palpa<\/strong> of Mukunda Sen; Ripu Malla's inscription on the Lumbini pillar<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td><strong>Khas kingdom<\/strong>, <strong>Sinja<\/strong> (Jumla) and <strong>Dullu<\/strong> (Dailekh); Baise states; birthplace of the Nepali language<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Kingdom of <strong>Doti<\/strong>; Khas rule reached the far west<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h33\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.3.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who built the stone Krishna Mandir of Patan?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Pratap Malla<\/li>\n<li>(b) Bhupatindra Malla<\/li>\n<li>(c) Siddhi Narsingh Malla<\/li>\n<li>(d) Mahendra Malla<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Siddhi Narsingh Malla of Lalitpur built it in 1637.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.3.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the kingdom with its last Malla king.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Kingdom<\/th>\n<th>Last king<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Kantipur<\/td>\n<td>1. Ranajit Malla<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Lalitpur<\/td>\n<td>2. Jaya Prakash Malla<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Bhaktapur<\/td>\n<td>3. Tej Narsingh Malla<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-2, B-3, C-1<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-3, B-2, C-1<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-1, B-3, C-2<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-2, B-1, C-3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Jaya Prakash Malla (Kantipur), Tej Narsingh Malla (Lalitpur), Ranajit Malla (Bhaktapur).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.3.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements about the Khas kingdom.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Dullu was its summer capital.<\/li>\n<li>Its capital, Sinja, lies in today's Jumla district.<\/li>\n<li>The name of Ripu Malla is cut on the Ashoka pillar at Lumbini.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Statement 1 is wrong: Dullu, in Dailekh, was the winter capital.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.3.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Simraungadh, the capital of the Karnat dynasty, lies in which present-day district?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Dhanusha<\/li>\n<li>(b) Bara<\/li>\n<li>(c) Mahottari<\/li>\n<li>(d) Rautahat<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Simraungadh is in Bara district of Madhesh Province. Nanyadeva founded the dynasty in 1097 AD.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.3.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Assertion (A):<\/strong> After the death of Yaksha Malla the Kathmandu Valley was divided into separate kingdoms.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reason (R):<\/strong> Jayasthiti Malla divided society into classes and castes by occupation.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Both A and R are true, and R explains A.<\/li>\n<li>(b) Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A.<\/li>\n<li>(c) A is true, but R is false.<\/li>\n<li>(d) A is false, but R is true.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Both statements are true, but the kingdom was divided among Yaksha Malla's heirs. The social reform of Jayasthiti Malla, a century earlier, was not the cause.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h34\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> Describe the reforms of Jayasthiti Malla.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> Jayasthiti Malla (ruled 1382–1395) brought order to a society disturbed by raids and weak rule. <strong>Social reform:<\/strong> he arranged society into four classes and 64 castes according to occupation, and fixed rules of dress, housing and conduct for each. <strong>Economic reform:<\/strong> he standardised weights and measures such as the <em>mana<\/em> and <em>pathi<\/em>, and graded land into four classes — <em>abal<\/em>, <em>doyam<\/em>, <em>sim<\/em> and <em>chahar<\/em> — so that land could be measured, sold and taxed fairly. <strong>Legal reform:<\/strong> he had written rules prepared with the help of learned Brahmins and fixed punishments for crimes. <strong>Cultural work:<\/strong> he encouraged literature and drama, and the Gopal Raj Vamshavali was written in his time. <strong>Assessment:<\/strong> his rules gave stability and helped trade and crafts, but they also fixed the caste system firmly, and its harmful effects lasted for centuries.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h35\">2.4 Unification to the Sugauli Treaty (एकीकरणदेखि सुगौली सन्धिसम्म)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h36\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>The making of modern Nepal is the most examined part of history: the steps of Prithvi Narayan Shah's campaign, the heroes of the war with the British, and the Sugauli Treaty that fixed today's east and west boundaries. The Karnali syllabus names the Shah dynasty.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h37\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>In the 18th century the land was divided into more than fifty small states. <strong>Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/strong>, king of the small hill state of Gorkha, began to join them into one country. He won the Kathmandu Valley in 1768–1769 and moved his capital there. His successors carried the border east to the <strong>Tista<\/strong> river and west to the <strong>Sutlej<\/strong>. This growth brought war with the British East India Company in 1814–1816. Nepal lost, and the <strong>Sugauli Treaty<\/strong> cut the country back to the land between the <strong>Mechi<\/strong> and the <strong>Mahakali<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h38\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Born on <strong>27 Poush 1779 BS (11 January 1723)<\/strong> in Gorkha; son of King Nara Bhupal Shah. Poush 27 is kept as <strong>Prithvi Jayanti and National Unity Day<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Became king of Gorkha in <strong>1799 BS (1743)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Nuwakot<\/strong> — first attack failed in 1743; <strong>won in 1744 (1801 BS)<\/strong>. It opened the trade road to Tibet and the way to the Valley. <strong>Kalu Pande<\/strong> was made <em>Kaji<\/em> (chief minister).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kirtipur<\/strong> — first attack <strong>1757<\/strong>: defeat, and <strong>Kalu Pande was killed<\/strong>. Second attack 1764: defeat. Third attack: Kirtipur fell in <strong>1766<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Makwanpur<\/strong> won in <strong>1762<\/strong>. In 1763 the Gorkhalis beat the army of Mir Qasim, Nawab of Bengal.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sindhuligadhi, 1767<\/strong> — the Gorkhalis defeated a British force under <strong>Captain Kinloch<\/strong>, sent to help the Malla king of Kantipur.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kantipur<\/strong> won on <strong>13 Ashoj 1825 BS (25 September 1768)<\/strong>, the day of <strong>Indra Jatra<\/strong>. <strong>Lalitpur<\/strong> followed in October 1768 and <strong>Bhaktapur<\/strong> in <strong>November 1769<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The capital moved from Gorkha to <strong>Kathmandu<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>In 1773–1774 the eastern Sen kingdoms of <strong>Chaudandi<\/strong> and <strong>Bijayapur<\/strong> were won.<\/li>\n<li>Died on <strong>1 Magh 1831 BS (11 January 1775)<\/strong> at <strong>Devighat, Nuwakot<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dibya Upadesh<\/strong> (दिव्य उपदेश, &quot;divine counsel&quot;) — his advice to the nation: Nepal is <strong>&quot;a yam between two boulders&quot;<\/strong> (दुई ढुङ्गाको तरुल); the country is a <strong>garden of four classes and thirty-six castes<\/strong>; keep friendship with both the northern and the southern neighbour; use home-made goods; punish both the giver and the taker of bribes.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>After Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kings: Pratap Singh Shah → Rana Bahadur Shah → Girvan Yuddha Bikram Shah → Rajendra Bikram Shah.<\/li>\n<li>Regents <strong>Rajendra Laxmi<\/strong> and <strong>Bahadur Shah<\/strong> carried on the campaign. The Chaubise and Baise states, including Jumla, were won, and <strong>Kumaon<\/strong> was taken in 1790.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Wars with Tibet and China, 1788–1792<\/strong> — ended by the <strong>Treaty of Kerung (1789)<\/strong> with Tibet and the <strong>Treaty of Betrawati (1792)<\/strong> after a Chinese army entered Nepal.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Bhimsen Thapa<\/strong> was <em>Mukhtiyar<\/em> (chief minister) from <strong>1806 to 1837<\/strong>. He is counted as the <strong>first Prime Minister of Nepal<\/strong>. He built the <strong>Dharahara<\/strong> tower. <strong>Mathbar Singh Thapa<\/strong> was later the first to use the title &quot;Prime Minister&quot;.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Amar Singh Thapa<\/strong> (the elder, <em>Bada Kaji<\/em>) took Garhwal in 1804 and reached <strong>Kangra<\/strong> and the <strong>Sutlej<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Greatest extent:<\/strong> from the <strong>Tista<\/strong> in the east to the <strong>Sutlej<\/strong> in the west.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Anglo-Nepal War, 1814–1816<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Causes:<\/strong> dispute over the border lands of <strong>Butwal and Syuraj<\/strong>; British wish for trade routes to Tibet and for the hill country; fear of Nepal's growth.<\/li>\n<li>War was declared by Governor-General <strong>Lord Hastings<\/strong> on <strong>1 November 1814<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sugauli Treaty:<\/strong> signed <strong>2 December 1815<\/strong>; ratified <strong>4 March 1816 (1872 BS)<\/strong>.\n<ul>\n<li>Nepal gave up the land <strong>east of the Mechi<\/strong> and <strong>west of the Mahakali<\/strong>, and most of the Terai — about <strong>one third<\/strong> of its territory.<\/li>\n<li>A <strong>British Resident<\/strong> was placed in Kathmandu.<\/li>\n<li>The treaty opened the way for the recruitment of Gorkha soldiers into British service.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li>A large part of the Terai came back in December 1816. The <strong>Naya Muluk<\/strong> — Banke, Bardiya, Kailali and Kanchanpur — came back in <strong>1860<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h39\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Steps of the unification campaign<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1743<\/td>\n<td>Prithvi Narayan Shah becomes king of Gorkha; first attack on Nuwakot fails<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1744<\/td>\n<td>Nuwakot won<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1757<\/td>\n<td>First attack on Kirtipur; Kalu Pande killed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1762<\/td>\n<td>Makwanpur won<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1766<\/td>\n<td>Kirtipur falls after the third attack<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1767<\/td>\n<td>Captain Kinloch's force beaten at Sindhuligadhi<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1768<\/td>\n<td>Kantipur (25 September) and Lalitpur won<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1769<\/td>\n<td>Bhaktapur won; capital moves to Kathmandu<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1773–1774<\/td>\n<td>Chaudandi and Bijayapur won<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1775<\/td>\n<td>Death of Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1790<\/td>\n<td>Kumaon won<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1804–1809<\/td>\n<td>Garhwal won; campaign to Kangra and the Sutlej<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Battles of the Anglo-Nepal War<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Battle<\/th>\n<th>Place today<\/th>\n<th>Nepali commander<\/th>\n<th>Result<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nalapani (Khalanga)<\/td>\n<td>Dehradun, India<\/td>\n<td><strong>Balbhadra Kunwar<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Long defence; British General Gillespie killed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jaithak<\/td>\n<td>Himachal Pradesh, India<\/td>\n<td>Ranajor Singh Thapa<\/td>\n<td>Nepali defence held<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deuthal<\/td>\n<td>Himachal Pradesh, India<\/td>\n<td><strong>Bhakti Thapa<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Bhakti Thapa killed, aged over 70<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Malaun<\/td>\n<td>Himachal Pradesh, India<\/td>\n<td><strong>Amar Singh Thapa<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Nepali surrender, 1815<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Jitgadh<\/td>\n<td>Butwal, Rupandehi<\/td>\n<td>Ujir Singh Thapa<\/td>\n<td>British attack beaten back<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Parsa<\/td>\n<td>Parsa<\/td>\n<td>Nepali border force<\/td>\n<td>British post destroyed, 1815<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Makwanpur<\/td>\n<td>Makawanpur<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>British advance under Ochterlony, 1816; treaty ratified<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h40\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Birth of Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/td>\n<td>27 Poush 1779 BS (11 January 1723)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nuwakot won<\/td>\n<td>1744 (1801 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Kantipur won<\/td>\n<td>13 Ashoj 1825 BS (25 September 1768)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Death of Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/td>\n<td>1 Magh 1831 BS (11 January 1775)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treaty of Kerung / Betrawati<\/td>\n<td>1789 / 1792<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bhimsen Thapa as Mukhtiyar<\/td>\n<td>1806–1837<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Anglo-Nepal War<\/td>\n<td>1814–1816<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sugauli Treaty signed / ratified<\/td>\n<td>2 December 1815 / 4 March 1816<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Naya Muluk returned<\/td>\n<td>1860<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h41\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;N – K – K – L – B&quot;:<\/strong> Nuwakot → Kirtipur → Kantipur → Lalitpur → Bhaktapur.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kirtipur took three attacks: 1757, 1764, 1766.<\/strong> Kalu Pande fell in the first.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Tista to Sutlej before Sugauli; Mechi to Mahakali after it.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Three heroes, three forts: &quot;Balbhadra at Nalapani, Bhakti at Deuthal, Amar Singh at Malaun.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Sugauli: &quot;signed '15, sealed '16.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h42\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The first victory of the campaign was Nuwakot (1744)<\/strong>, not Kirtipur or Kathmandu.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kalu Pande died at Kirtipur<\/strong>, not at Nuwakot.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kantipur fell on the day of Indra Jatra.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Tista–Sutlej<\/strong> limit belongs to the time <strong>after<\/strong> Prithvi Narayan Shah. In his own lifetime the kingdom reached the east but not the far west.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Two men named Amar Singh Thapa:<\/strong> the elder (<em>Bada Kaji<\/em>) commanded in the west; the other was the father of Bhimsen Thapa.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sugauli did not return the Naya Muluk.<\/strong> That came in 1860, under Jung Bahadur.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Bhimsen Thapa<\/strong> is called the first Prime Minister, but his title was <em>Mukhtiyar<\/em>.<\/li>\n<li>Nalapani, Jaithak, Deuthal and Malaun are <strong>in India today<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h43\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Points from this period<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Chaudandi and Bijayapur won in 1773–1774; the <strong>Mechi<\/strong> became the eastern boundary in 1816; land up to the Tista was lost<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td><strong>Parsa<\/strong> fighting in the war of 1814–1816; the treaty takes its name from <strong>Sugauli<\/strong>, a town in Bihar not far from the border; much of the Terai, including this region, returned in December 1816<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Nuwakot, Kirtipur, Kantipur, Lalitpur, Bhaktapur; Makwanpur; <strong>Sindhuligadhi<\/strong> (1767); Devighat<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td><strong>Gorkha<\/strong>, birthplace of Prithvi Narayan Shah and starting point of the campaign; the Chaubise states were joined later<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Palpa joined to Nepal in the early 19th century; <strong>Butwal and Syuraj<\/strong> were the immediate cause of the war; <strong>Jitgadh<\/strong> (Butwal); Banke and Bardiya returned in 1860<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td><strong>Jumla<\/strong> and the other Baise states joined to Nepal under the regency of Bahadur Shah<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Doti joined to Nepal; base of the western campaign; the <strong>Mahakali<\/strong> became the western boundary in 1816; Kailali and Kanchanpur returned in 1860<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h44\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.4.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which was the first place won by Prithvi Narayan Shah in his unification campaign?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Kirtipur<\/li>\n<li>(b) Makwanpur<\/li>\n<li>(c) Kantipur<\/li>\n<li>(d) Nuwakot<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Nuwakot was won in 1744 (1801 BS), after a failed attack in 1743.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.4.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the events in order of time, earliest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Conquest of Kantipur<\/li>\n<li>Conquest of Nuwakot<\/li>\n<li>Ratification of the Sugauli Treaty<\/li>\n<li>Death of Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2 – 1 – 4 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 – 2 – 4 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2 – 1 – 3 – 4<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Nuwakot 1744, Kantipur 1768, death of the king 1775, Sugauli Treaty ratified 1816.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.4.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the battle with the Nepali commander.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Battle<\/th>\n<th>Commander<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Nalapani<\/td>\n<td>1. Bhakti Thapa<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Deuthal<\/td>\n<td>2. Ujir Singh Thapa<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Malaun<\/td>\n<td>3. Balbhadra Kunwar<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Jitgadh<\/td>\n<td>4. Amar Singh Thapa<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-1, B-3, C-4, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Nalapani–Balbhadra Kunwar, Deuthal–Bhakti Thapa, Malaun–Amar Singh Thapa, Jitgadh–Ujir Singh Thapa.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.4.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements about the Sugauli Treaty.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>It was signed in 1815 and ratified in 1816.<\/li>\n<li>It made the Mechi and the Mahakali the boundary rivers of Nepal.<\/li>\n<li>It returned Banke, Bardiya, Kailali and Kanchanpur to Nepal.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — The four districts of the Naya Muluk were returned in 1860, not by the Sugauli Treaty.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.4.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In the Dibya Upadesh, Prithvi Narayan Shah compared Nepal to:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) a yam between two boulders<\/li>\n<li>(b) a bridge between two rivers<\/li>\n<li>(c) a lamp between two winds<\/li>\n<li>(d) a fort between two armies<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — &quot;दुई ढुङ्गाको तरुल&quot;: a small country between two large neighbours must keep a careful balance.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h45\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> What were the causes and results of the Anglo-Nepal War?<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> <strong>Causes:<\/strong> (1) The border between Nepal and the East India Company's lands was unclear, and both claimed Butwal and Syuraj; this was the immediate cause. (2) Nepal's rapid growth from the Tista to the Sutlej alarmed the Company. (3) The Company wanted a trade route to Tibet through Nepal and the cool hill country. (4) Earlier clashes, such as the defeat of Captain Kinloch in 1767, had left mistrust. <strong>Course:<\/strong> the war began on 1 November 1814. Nepali forces fought bravely at Nalapani, Jaithak, Deuthal and Jitgadh, but lost Malaun, and a British army advanced towards Makwanpur. <strong>Results:<\/strong> (1) Nepal accepted the Sugauli Treaty, signed on 2 December 1815 and ratified on 4 March 1816. (2) Nepal lost about one third of its land; the Mechi and the Mahakali became the boundaries. (3) A British Resident came to Kathmandu. (4) Gorkha soldiers began to serve in the British army. (5) The age of expansion ended, and court politics turned inward, which led in time to the rise of the Ranas.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h46\">2.5 Rana period (राणाकाल), 1846–1951<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h47\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>For 104 years the Rana prime ministers held all power and the kings had none. Questions ask who did what — which prime minister founded the first newspaper, college or bank — and about the movements and martyrs that ended the regime.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h48\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>After the death of Bhimsen Thapa the court was full of plots. On one night in 1846 <strong>Jung Bahadur Kunwar<\/strong> killed his rivals in the <strong>Kot Massacre<\/strong> and became Prime Minister. He made the post <strong>hereditary in his own family<\/strong>, passing from brother to brother. The Rana family ruled until 1951. They kept Nepal independent and made some reforms, but they kept the people without rights or schooling, and the country closed to the world.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h49\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Rise of Jung Bahadur<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kot Massacre (कोतपर्व): 14 September 1846 (2 Ashoj 1903 BS)<\/strong>, at the Kot (armoury court) near Hanuman Dhoka. It followed the murder of the queen's favourite, Gagan Singh. Jung Bahadur became Prime Minister and Commander-in-Chief.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Bhandarkhal Parva (1846):<\/strong> a plot against Jung Bahadur failed. <strong>Alau Parva (1847):<\/strong> King Rajendra's attempt to return failed at Alau in Parsa; <strong>Surendra<\/strong> became king.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Visit to Britain and France, 1850–1851<\/strong> — the first by a ruler of Nepal.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Muluki Ain, 1910 BS (1854)<\/strong> — the first written national legal code.<\/li>\n<li><strong>1856:<\/strong> a royal order gave Jung Bahadur the title <strong>Maharaja of Kaski and Lamjung<\/strong> and made the prime ministership hereditary in his family.<\/li>\n<li><strong>War with Tibet, 1855–1856<\/strong> — ended by the <strong>Treaty of Thapathali (1856)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>1857:<\/strong> Jung Bahadur helped the British to put down the uprising in India. In return the <strong>Naya Muluk<\/strong> came back in <strong>1860<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Jung Bahadur died in 1877.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Later Rana prime ministers<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Ranodip Singh<\/strong> — murdered in <strong>1885 (1942 BS)<\/strong> by his nephews, the Shamsher brothers.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Bir Shamsher<\/strong> — <strong>Bir Hospital<\/strong> (1947 BS), piped drinking water, the clock tower (Ghantaghar).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dev Shamsher<\/strong> — ruled only <strong>114 days<\/strong> in 1901. Started the <strong>Gorkhapatra<\/strong> (24 Baisakh 1958 BS; 6 May 1901) and opened schools for the public. Removed by his brother Chandra.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Chandra Shamsher<\/strong> (1901–1929):\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Pharping hydropower<\/strong> (1911; 1968 BS);<\/li>\n<li><strong>Tri-Chandra College<\/strong> (1975 BS; 1918), the first college;<\/li>\n<li><strong>abolition of Sati<\/strong> on 25 Ashad 1977 BS (8 July 1920);<\/li>\n<li><strong>abolition of slavery<\/strong>, announced on 14 Mangsir 1981 BS (28 November 1924), in force from 1 Baisakh 1982 BS (13 April 1925);<\/li>\n<li><strong>treaty with Britain, 21 December 1923 (6 Poush 1980 BS)<\/strong>, in which Britain recognised Nepal's independence;<\/li>\n<li><strong>Singha Durbar<\/strong>; the first railway (Raxaul–Amlekhgunj) and ropeway;<\/li>\n<li>division of the Rana family into A, B and C classes.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Bhim Shamsher<\/strong> (1929–1932).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Juddha Shamsher<\/strong> (1932–1945): relief and rebuilding after the <strong>earthquake of 2 Magh 1990 BS (15 January 1934)<\/strong>; <strong>Biratnagar Jute Mill<\/strong> (1936), the first modern factory; <strong>Nepal Bank Limited<\/strong> (30 Kartik 1994 BS; 15 November 1937), the first bank; execution of the <strong>four martyrs<\/strong> (Magh 1997 BS).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Padma Shamsher<\/strong> (1945–1948): announced the <strong>Government of Nepal Act, 2004<\/strong> on <strong>13 Magh 2004 BS (26 January 1948)<\/strong> — Nepal's <strong>first constitution<\/strong>. It was never properly put into force. He resigned in 1948.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mohan Shamsher<\/strong> (1948–1951): the last Rana Prime Minister. He signed the <strong>Treaty of Peace and Friendship with India on 31 July 1950 (16 Shrawan 2007 BS)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Movements against the Ranas<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Lakhan Thapa Magar<\/strong> of Gorkha rose against Jung Bahadur and was hanged in <strong>1877 (1933 BS)<\/strong>. He is honoured as the <strong>first martyr<\/strong> of Nepal.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Makai Parva (1977 BS):<\/strong> Krishna Lal Adhikari was jailed for his book <em>Makaiko Kheti<\/em> (&quot;The Cultivation of Maize&quot;) and died in prison.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Library Parva (1987 BS):<\/strong> young men were punished for planning a public library.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Prachanda Gorkha (1988 BS):<\/strong> a secret group that planned to overthrow the Ranas.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Nepal Praja Parishad<\/strong> — founded on <strong>20 Jestha 1993 BS (2 June 1936)<\/strong>; the <strong>first political party<\/strong>; president <strong>Tanka Prasad Acharya<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>The four martyrs of 1997 BS (January 1941):<\/strong>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Shukraraj Shastri<\/strong> — hanged, 10 Magh;<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dharma Bhakta Mathema<\/strong> — hanged, 12 Magh;<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dashrath Chand<\/strong> and <strong>Ganga Lal Shrestha<\/strong> — shot, 15 Magh.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Martyrs' Day<\/strong> is kept on <strong>Magh 16<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>Biratnagar Jute Mill strike:<\/strong> 21 Falgun 2003 BS (4 March 1947) — the first large labour movement.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Jayatu Sanskritam (2004 BS):<\/strong> the first student movement.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Parties:<\/strong> Nepali National Congress (12 Magh 2003 BS; 25 January 1947). <strong>Nepali Congress<\/strong> was formed on <strong>27 Chaitra 2006 BS (9 April 1950)<\/strong> by its union with the Nepal Democratic Congress. The <strong>Communist Party of Nepal<\/strong> was founded on <strong>10 Baisakh 2006 BS (22 April 1949)<\/strong> by <strong>Pushpa Lal Shrestha<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h50\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>The Rana prime ministers<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.<\/th>\n<th>Prime Minister<\/th>\n<th>In office (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Remembered for<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>Jung Bahadur<\/td>\n<td>1846–1877<\/td>\n<td>Kot Massacre; Muluki Ain 1910; visit to Europe; Naya Muluk<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>Ranodip Singh<\/td>\n<td>1877–1885<\/td>\n<td>Murdered in 1885<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>Bir Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>1885–1901<\/td>\n<td>Bir Hospital; drinking water; clock tower<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>Dev Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>1901<\/td>\n<td>114 days; Gorkhapatra; public schools<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>Chandra Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>1901–1929<\/td>\n<td>Sati and slavery abolished; Tri-Chandra College; treaty of 1923<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>Bhim Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>1929–1932<\/td>\n<td>Short rule<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>Juddha Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>1932–1945<\/td>\n<td>Earthquake of 1990 BS; Nepal Bank; jute mill; four martyrs<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>8<\/td>\n<td>Padma Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>1945–1948<\/td>\n<td>Government of Nepal Act, 2004<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>9<\/td>\n<td>Mohan Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>1948–1951<\/td>\n<td>Last Rana Prime Minister; treaty of 1950 with India<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>⚖️ Textbooks usually count <strong>nine<\/strong> Rana prime ministers. Lists that add Jung Bahadur's brothers Bam Bahadur and Krishna Bahadur, who held the post briefly in 1856–1857, count ten or eleven.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Movements and organisations<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year (BS)<\/th>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<th>Key name<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1933<\/td>\n<td>Revolt and execution of Lakhan Thapa<\/td>\n<td>Lakhan Thapa Magar<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1977<\/td>\n<td>Makai Parva<\/td>\n<td>Krishna Lal Adhikari<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1987<\/td>\n<td>Library Parva<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1988<\/td>\n<td>Prachanda Gorkha<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1993<\/td>\n<td>Nepal Praja Parishad founded<\/td>\n<td>Tanka Prasad Acharya<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1997<\/td>\n<td>Four martyrs executed<\/td>\n<td>Shukraraj Shastri, Dharma Bhakta Mathema, Dashrath Chand, Ganga Lal Shrestha<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2003<\/td>\n<td>Nepali National Congress; Biratnagar Jute Mill strike<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2004<\/td>\n<td>Jayatu Sanskritam; Government of Nepal Act<\/td>\n<td>Padma Shamsher<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2006<\/td>\n<td>Communist Party of Nepal; Nepali Congress<\/td>\n<td>Pushpa Lal Shrestha<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h51\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Kot Massacre<\/td>\n<td>14 September 1846 (2 Ashoj 1903 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Muluki Ain<\/td>\n<td>1910 BS (1854)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treaty of Thapathali<\/td>\n<td>1856<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gorkhapatra<\/td>\n<td>24 Baisakh 1958 BS (6 May 1901)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tri-Chandra College<\/td>\n<td>1975 BS (1918)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sati abolished<\/td>\n<td>25 Ashad 1977 BS (8 July 1920)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Treaty with Britain<\/td>\n<td>21 December 1923<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Slavery abolished<\/td>\n<td>announced 14 Mangsir 1981 BS; in force 1 Baisakh 1982 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepal Praja Parishad<\/td>\n<td>20 Jestha 1993 BS (2 June 1936)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepal Bank Limited<\/td>\n<td>30 Kartik 1994 BS (15 November 1937)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Martyrs' Day<\/td>\n<td>Magh 16<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Government of Nepal Act, 2004<\/td>\n<td>13 Magh 2004 BS (26 January 1948)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Length of Rana rule<\/td>\n<td>104 years (1846–1951)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h52\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Order of the Rana prime ministers: &quot;J – R – B – D – C – B – J – P – M&quot;<\/strong> — &quot;<strong>J<\/strong>ust <strong>R<\/strong>emember: <strong>B<\/strong>ir, <strong>D<\/strong>ev, <strong>C<\/strong>handra, <strong>B<\/strong>him, <strong>J<\/strong>uddha, <strong>P<\/strong>adma, <strong>M<\/strong>ohan.&quot;<\/li>\n<li><strong>Chandra's &quot;S – S – C&quot;:<\/strong> <strong>S<\/strong>ati ended, <strong>S<\/strong>lavery ended, <strong>C<\/strong>ollege opened.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dev = &quot;114 days, one newspaper&quot;.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Four martyrs: &quot;two hanged, two shot&quot;<\/strong> — Shukraraj and Dharma Bhakta hanged; Dashrath and Ganga Lal shot.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;93 – 97 – 2003 – 2006&quot;:<\/strong> Praja Parishad, the martyrs, the National Congress, the two great parties.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h53\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Tanka Prasad Acharya was not executed.<\/strong> As a Brahmin he was jailed for life; he later became Prime Minister.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Lakhan Thapa is the first martyr<\/strong>; the &quot;four martyrs&quot; came 64 years later.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Gorkhapatra was started by Dev Shamsher<\/strong>, not Chandra Shamsher.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Durbar High School (1910 BS)<\/strong> was the first school; <strong>Tri-Chandra (1975 BS)<\/strong> was the first college.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Government of Nepal Act, 2004<\/strong> is the first constitution, but it never worked in practice.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Two treaties with Britain:<\/strong> Sugauli (1816) and the friendship treaty of 1923. Only the second recognised Nepal as fully independent.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>1950 treaty with India<\/strong> was signed by a Rana, Mohan Shamsher, a few months before the regime fell.<\/li>\n<li>The number of Rana prime ministers is counted in more than one way ⚖️.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h54\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Points from this period<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td><strong>Biratnagar Jute Mill<\/strong> (1936) and its strike of 2003 BS, where the labour and democratic movements met<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td><strong>Alau Parva<\/strong> at Alau, Parsa (1847); Raxaul–Amlekhgunj railway and the old trade road through <strong>Birgunj<\/strong>; Janakpur–Jaynagar railway of the 1930s<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Kot Massacre; Singha Durbar; Tri-Chandra College; Bir Hospital; the places of execution of the four martyrs in Kathmandu<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td><strong>Lakhan Thapa Magar<\/strong> of Gorkha, first martyr; Jung Bahadur's title &quot;Maharaja of Kaski and Lamjung&quot;<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td><strong>Banke and Bardiya<\/strong> returned as Naya Muluk (1860); rediscovery of the Lumbini pillar (1896) by Khadga Shamsher, a Rana governor of Palpa<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>Administered through governors (बडाहाकिम) sent from Kathmandu<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td><strong>Kailali and Kanchanpur<\/strong> returned as Naya Muluk (1860); the martyr <strong>Dashrath Chand<\/strong> was born in Baitadi<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h55\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.5.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In which year did the Kot Massacre take place?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1816 AD<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1837 AD<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1846 AD<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1856 AD<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — 14 September 1846 (1903 BS). It brought Jung Bahadur to power.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.5.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the Rana Prime Minister with the work linked with him.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Prime Minister<\/th>\n<th>Work<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Dev Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>1. Nepal Bank Limited<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Chandra Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>2. Bir Hospital<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Juddha Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>3. Gorkhapatra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Bir Shamsher<\/td>\n<td>4. Abolition of slavery<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Dev Shamsher–Gorkhapatra, Chandra Shamsher–abolition of slavery, Juddha Shamsher–Nepal Bank Limited, Bir Shamsher–Bir Hospital.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.5.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the Rana prime ministers in the order in which they held office.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Chandra Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>Bir Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>Juddha Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>Dev Shamsher<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2 – 1 – 4 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(c) 4 – 2 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2 – 4 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Bir (1885–1901), Dev (1901), Chandra (1901–1929), Juddha (1932–1945).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.5.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Three of these men were executed in Magh 1997 BS. Which one was not?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Tanka Prasad Acharya<\/li>\n<li>(b) Shukraraj Shastri<\/li>\n<li>(c) Dharma Bhakta Mathema<\/li>\n<li>(d) Ganga Lal Shrestha<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Tanka Prasad Acharya, president of the Nepal Praja Parishad, was sentenced to life in prison. The fourth martyr was Dashrath Chand.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.5.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which was the first political party of Nepal?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Nepali Congress<\/li>\n<li>(b) Nepal Praja Parishad<\/li>\n<li>(c) Communist Party of Nepal<\/li>\n<li>(d) Prachanda Gorkha<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — The Nepal Praja Parishad was founded on 20 Jestha 1993 BS (2 June 1936). Prachanda Gorkha was a secret group, not a party.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h56\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> Mention the main reforms of Chandra Shamsher.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> Chandra Shamsher ruled from 1901 to 1929, the longest of the later Ranas. <strong>Social reforms:<\/strong> he abolished the custom of Sati on 25 Ashad 1977 BS (8 July 1920) and announced the end of slavery on 14 Mangsir 1981 BS (28 November 1924), freeing tens of thousands of slaves from 1 Baisakh 1982 BS. <strong>Education:<\/strong> he opened Tri-Chandra College in 1975 BS, the first college in the country. <strong>Development:<\/strong> he built the Pharping hydropower station (1911), Singha Durbar, the first railway from Raxaul to Amlekhgunj, a ropeway and canals. <strong>Foreign relations:<\/strong> by the treaty of 21 December 1923 Britain recognised Nepal as an independent state. <strong>Limits:<\/strong> he removed his liberal brother Dev Shamsher, divided the Rana family into classes, and kept strict control over the people. His reforms were real, but they also served to keep Rana rule strong.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h57\">2.6 The revolution of 2007 BS and the decade to 2017 BS (२००७ सालको क्रान्ति र त्यसपछिको दशक)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h58\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>The Karnali syllabus names &quot;events since 2007 BS&quot;. Democracy Day, the first election, the first elected Prime Minister and the royal takeover of 2017 are tested every way: dates, order and matching.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h59\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>By 1950 three forces stood against the Ranas: the <strong>King<\/strong>, who wanted his powers back; the <strong>political parties<\/strong>, who wanted democracy; and newly independent <strong>India<\/strong>, which supported change. King Tribhuvan left his palace, the Nepali Congress took up arms, and in a few months the Ranas gave way. Democracy was announced on <strong>7 Falgun 2007<\/strong>. The next ten years saw many short governments, the first general election in 2015 BS, an elected government under B.P. Koirala — and then its dismissal by King Mahendra on <strong>1 Poush 2017<\/strong>.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h60\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>The revolution<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>21 Kartik 2007 (6 November 1950):<\/strong> King <strong>Tribhuvan<\/strong> and his family took refuge in the <strong>Indian Embassy<\/strong> in Kathmandu. A few days later they flew to Delhi.<\/li>\n<li><strong>22 Kartik 2007 (7 November 1950):<\/strong> the Ranas placed his young grandson <strong>Gyanendra<\/strong> on the throne. No foreign country recognised him.<\/li>\n<li>The Nepali Congress <strong>Mukti Sena<\/strong> (Liberation Army) attacked from the border. <strong>Birgunj<\/strong> was among the first towns taken.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Delhi Agreement<\/strong> (early 1951) — a three-sided settlement among the King, the Ranas and the Nepali Congress, reached with India's help.<\/li>\n<li><strong>4 Falgun 2007 (15 February 1951):<\/strong> King Tribhuvan returned to Kathmandu.<\/li>\n<li><strong>7 Falgun 2007 (18 February 1951):<\/strong> the King proclaimed the end of Rana rule and the start of democracy. This is <strong>Democracy Day<\/strong> (प्रजातन्त्र दिवस).<\/li>\n<li>A joint cabinet was formed: <strong>Mohan Shamsher<\/strong> as Prime Minister, with <strong>five Rana and five Nepali Congress ministers<\/strong>. <strong>B.P. Koirala<\/strong> was Home Minister.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Interim Government of Nepal Act, 2007<\/strong> — the second constitution — was issued in Chaitra 2007 (1951).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The decade of trial, 2008–2015 BS<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1 Mangsir 2008 (16 November 1951):<\/strong> <strong>Matrika Prasad Koirala<\/strong> became the <strong>first Prime Minister from among the people<\/strong> (not a Rana).<\/li>\n<li><strong>21 Magh 2008 (4 February 1952):<\/strong> the <strong>first budget<\/strong> of Nepal was presented.<\/li>\n<li><strong>30 Falgun 2011 (13 March 1955):<\/strong> King Tribhuvan died in Zurich. <strong>King Mahendra<\/strong> followed him.<\/li>\n<li><strong>17 Shrawan 2012 (1 August 1955):<\/strong> diplomatic relations with <strong>China<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>29 Mangsir 2012 (14 December 1955):<\/strong> Nepal became a member of the <strong>United Nations<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>2013 BS (1956):<\/strong> the <strong>First Five-Year Plan<\/strong> began; <strong>Nepal Rastra Bank<\/strong> was set up on 14 Baisakh 2013 (26 April 1956); the <strong>Supreme Court<\/strong> was set up.<\/li>\n<li>Prime ministers of these years included <strong>Tanka Prasad Acharya<\/strong> (2012–2014 BS) and <strong>Dr K.I. Singh<\/strong> (2014 BS). <strong>Subarna Shamsher<\/strong> led the government that held the election.<\/li>\n<li>Institutions of the decade: <strong>Radio Nepal<\/strong> (20 Chaitra 2007; 2 April 1951), <strong>Public Service Commission<\/strong> (1 Ashad 2008; 15 June 1951), <strong>Tribhuvan University<\/strong> (2016 BS).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The first election and the first elected government<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1 Falgun 2015 (12 February 1959):<\/strong> King Mahendra gave the <strong>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal, 2015<\/strong> — the third constitution. It set up a parliament of two houses.<\/li>\n<li><strong>First general election:<\/strong> voting began on <strong>7 Falgun 2015 (18 February 1959)<\/strong> and went on in stages for about six weeks.\n<ul>\n<li><strong>109 seats<\/strong> in the House of Representatives.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Nepali Congress 74<\/strong>, Gorkha Parishad 19, Samyukta Prajatantra Party 5, Communist Party of Nepal 4, the two Praja Parishad groups 3, independents 4.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<\/li>\n<li><strong>13 Jestha 2016 (27 May 1959):<\/strong> <strong>Bishweshwar Prasad (B.P.) Koirala<\/strong> became the <strong>first elected Prime Minister<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>His government abolished <strong>birta<\/strong> (tax-free land grants), began to end the small vassal principalities (रजौटा), and widened foreign relations.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dwarika Devi Thakurani<\/strong>, the only woman elected in 2015 BS, became the first woman minister.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The royal takeover<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>1 Poush 2017 (15 December 1960):<\/strong> King Mahendra used <strong>Article 55<\/strong> (emergency powers) of the 2015 Constitution. He dismissed the government, dissolved parliament and jailed B.P. Koirala and other leaders.<\/li>\n<li><strong>22 Poush 2017 (5 January 1961):<\/strong> political parties were banned.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h61\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>From revolution to takeover<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date (BS)<\/th>\n<th>Date (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>21 Kartik 2007<\/td>\n<td>6 November 1950<\/td>\n<td>King Tribhuvan enters the Indian Embassy<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7 Falgun 2007<\/td>\n<td>18 February 1951<\/td>\n<td>Democracy proclaimed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Mangsir 2008<\/td>\n<td>16 November 1951<\/td>\n<td>M.P. Koirala becomes Prime Minister<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>30 Falgun 2011<\/td>\n<td>13 March 1955<\/td>\n<td>Death of King Tribhuvan<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29 Mangsir 2012<\/td>\n<td>14 December 1955<\/td>\n<td>United Nations membership<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14 Baisakh 2013<\/td>\n<td>26 April 1956<\/td>\n<td>Nepal Rastra Bank<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Falgun 2015<\/td>\n<td>12 February 1959<\/td>\n<td>Constitution of 2015<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7 Falgun 2015<\/td>\n<td>18 February 1959<\/td>\n<td>First general election begins<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13 Jestha 2016<\/td>\n<td>27 May 1959<\/td>\n<td>B.P. Koirala becomes Prime Minister<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Poush 2017<\/td>\n<td>15 December 1960<\/td>\n<td>Royal takeover<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Result of the first general election (109 seats)<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Party<\/th>\n<th>Seats<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepali Congress<\/td>\n<td>74<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepal Rashtrabadi Gorkha Parishad<\/td>\n<td>19<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Samyukta Prajatantra Party<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communist Party of Nepal<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepal Praja Parishad (two groups)<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Independents<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>109<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h62\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Democracy Day<\/td>\n<td>Falgun 7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Members of the first joint cabinet<\/td>\n<td>10 (5 Rana + 5 Congress)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First budget<\/td>\n<td>21 Magh 2008<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>United Nations membership<\/td>\n<td>14 December 1955<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First Five-Year Plan<\/td>\n<td>from 2013 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seats in the first election<\/td>\n<td>109<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Seats won by the Nepali Congress<\/td>\n<td>74<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>First elected Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>13 Jestha 2016<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Royal takeover<\/td>\n<td>1 Poush 2017; Article 55<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h63\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;7 – 7&quot;:<\/strong> democracy came on <strong>Falgun 7<\/strong> of 200<strong>7<\/strong>; the first election began on <strong>Falgun 7<\/strong> of 2015.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Three sides, one Delhi&quot;:<\/strong> King, Ranas, Congress — the Delhi Agreement.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;5 + 5&quot;:<\/strong> the first cabinet had five Ranas and five Congress ministers.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;74 of 109&quot;:<\/strong> a two-thirds win for the Nepali Congress.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Poush 1&quot;:<\/strong> the takeover was on 1 Poush 2017, and the Panchayat constitution came on 1 Poush 2019.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h64\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Democracy Day (Falgun 7)<\/strong> is not <strong>Loktantra Day (Baisakh 11)<\/strong> or <strong>Republic Day (Jestha 15)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>M.P. Koirala<\/strong> was the first Prime Minister from the people; <strong>B.P. Koirala<\/strong> was the first <strong>elected<\/strong> Prime Minister. They were brothers.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mohan Shamsher<\/strong> was Prime Minister both before and after the revolution, until Mangsir 2008.<\/li>\n<li>The first election was held under the <strong>2015 Constitution<\/strong>, not the Interim Act of 2007.<\/li>\n<li>The takeover of <strong>2017<\/strong> was by <strong>King Mahendra<\/strong>; the takeover of <strong>2061<\/strong> was by King Gyanendra.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Gyanendra was king twice:<\/strong> as a child in 2007 BS, and from 2058 to 2065 BS.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h65\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Points from this period<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td><strong>Biratnagar<\/strong>, home of the Koirala family and a base of the Nepali Congress; fighting in the eastern hills and Terai in 2007 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td><strong>Birgunj<\/strong> taken by the Mukti Sena in Kartik 2007; border towns were the entry points of the armed movement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Indian Embassy in Kathmandu; return of the King; proclamation of 7 Falgun; parliament of 2016 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>The local raja of <strong>Mustang<\/strong> was one of the vassal chiefs whose powers were ended by law from 2017 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td><strong>Bhairahawa<\/strong> and <strong>Palpa<\/strong> were centres of the 2007 movement in the west<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td><strong>Jajarkot<\/strong> and <strong>Salyan<\/strong> had vassal rajas whose powers were ended by law from 2017 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td><strong>Bhimdatta Panta<\/strong> led a peasants' movement in the far west and was killed in 2010 BS; <strong>Dr K.I. Singh<\/strong> of Doti became Prime Minister in 2014 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h66\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.6.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>On which date is Democracy Day (प्रजातन्त्र दिवस) observed?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Falgun 7<\/li>\n<li>(b) Baisakh 11<\/li>\n<li>(c) Jestha 15<\/li>\n<li>(d) Ashoj 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — King Tribhuvan proclaimed democracy on 7 Falgun 2007 (18 February 1951).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.6.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the events in order of time, earliest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>First general election<\/li>\n<li>Proclamation of democracy<\/li>\n<li>Dismissal of the B.P. Koirala government<\/li>\n<li>King Tribhuvan takes refuge in the Indian Embassy<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 4 – 2 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(c) 4 – 1 – 2 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 4 – 2 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Kartik 2007, Falgun 2007, Falgun 2015, Poush 2017.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.6.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements about the general election of 2015 BS.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The House of Representatives had 109 seats.<\/li>\n<li>Tanka Prasad Acharya became the first elected Prime Minister.<\/li>\n<li>The Nepali Congress won 74 seats.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Statement 2 is wrong: the first elected Prime Minister was B.P. Koirala.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.6.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who was the first Prime Minister of Nepal from among the people, after the end of Rana rule?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) B.P. Koirala<\/li>\n<li>(b) Tanka Prasad Acharya<\/li>\n<li>(c) Dr K.I. Singh<\/li>\n<li>(d) Matrika Prasad Koirala<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Matrika Prasad Koirala took office on 1 Mangsir 2008 (16 November 1951).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.6.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the event with its year.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<th>Year (BS)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Delhi Agreement<\/td>\n<td>1. 2016<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. United Nations membership<\/td>\n<td>2. 2013<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Nepal Rastra Bank<\/td>\n<td>3. 2007<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. B.P. Koirala becomes Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>4. 2012<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Delhi Agreement 2007, United Nations 2012, Nepal Rastra Bank 2013, B.P. Koirala 2016.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h67\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> What were the causes of the revolution of 2007 BS?<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> <strong>(1) Autocratic family rule:<\/strong> for 104 years the Ranas kept all power, wealth and high posts in one family and gave the people no rights. <strong>(2) Powerless monarchy:<\/strong> the kings were kept like prisoners in the palace; King Tribhuvan quietly supported the movement. <strong>(3) Political awakening:<\/strong> the Nepal Praja Parishad, the sacrifice of the four martyrs in 1997 BS, and later the Nepali Congress and the Communist Party organised the people. <strong>(4) Education and outside contact:<\/strong> Nepalis who studied or worked in India, and soldiers who returned from two world wars, brought new ideas. <strong>(5) Change in India:<\/strong> India became independent in 1947; the Ranas lost their British protector, and the new Indian government favoured reform. <strong>(6) Division among the Ranas:<\/strong> the A, B and C classes left many Ranas discontented, and some of them financed the movement. Together these forces made the King, the parties and India act at the same time in 2007 BS, and the regime fell within about three months.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h68\">2.7 Panchayat period (पञ्चायतकाल), 2017–2046 BS<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h69\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Thirty years of party-less rule shaped today's administration: the zones and districts, the legal code, land reform and the highways all date from this time. The referendum of 2037 BS and the Zone of Peace proposal are frequent questions.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h70\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>After the takeover of 2017 BS, King Mahendra said that the parliamentary system did not suit Nepal. He introduced the <strong>Panchayat system<\/strong>: councils (<em>panchayat<\/em>) at village, district and national levels, with <strong>no political parties<\/strong> and with real power in the hands of the King. King Birendra continued it after 2028 BS. A students' movement forced a <strong>referendum<\/strong> in 2037 BS, which the Panchayat side narrowly won. Ten years later the <strong>People's Movement of 2046<\/strong> ended the system.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h71\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>The system<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Constitution of Nepal, 2019<\/strong> — given by King Mahendra on <strong>1 Poush 2019 (16 December 1962)<\/strong>; the fourth constitution.<\/li>\n<li>Sovereignty lay with the <strong>King<\/strong>. Parties were banned. The national legislature was the <strong>Rastriya Panchayat<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>&quot;Class organisations&quot; of peasants, workers, youth, women and ex-soldiers replaced parties.<\/li>\n<li>The country was divided into <strong>14 zones and 75 districts<\/strong> on <strong>1 Baisakh 2018 (13 April 1961)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Development regions:<\/strong> four in 2029 BS; the fifth (Far-Western) was added in 2037 BS.<\/li>\n<li>The 2019 Constitution was amended <strong>three times: 2023, 2032 and 2037 BS<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Main works and events<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Muluki Ain, 2020<\/strong> — a new legal code, in force from <strong>1 Bhadra 2020 (17 August 1963)<\/strong>. It ended legal discrimination by caste.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Land Reform Act, 2021<\/strong> — fixed a ceiling on land-holding and gave rights to tenants.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Back-to-the-Village National Campaign<\/strong> (गाउँ फर्क राष्ट्रिय अभियान), from 2024 BS.<\/li>\n<li><strong>New Education System Plan, 2028.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Roads:<\/strong> the East–West (Mahendra) Highway was begun; the Kodari (Araniko) Highway to the Chinese border was built.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Foreign policy:<\/strong> founding member of the <strong>Non-Aligned Movement (1961)<\/strong>; <strong>boundary treaty with China, 5 October 1961<\/strong>; non-permanent member of the United Nations Security Council in <strong>1969–1970<\/strong> and <strong>1988–1989<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>17 Magh 2028 (31 January 1972):<\/strong> King Mahendra died at Bharatpur. <strong>King Birendra<\/strong> followed; he was crowned on <strong>12 Falgun 2031 (24 February 1975)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Zone of Peace<\/strong> (शान्ति क्षेत्र): King Birendra proposed, at the time of his coronation in 2031 BS, that Nepal be declared a Zone of Peace. <strong>116 countries<\/strong> supported it.<\/li>\n<li><strong>SAARC<\/strong> was founded on 8 December 1985; its <strong>secretariat<\/strong> was set up in <strong>Kathmandu<\/strong> in 1987.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The referendum<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>A students' movement began in Chaitra 2035 – Baisakh 2036 (1979).<\/li>\n<li><strong>10 Jestha 2036 (24 May 1979):<\/strong> King Birendra announced a <strong>national referendum<\/strong> (जनमत संग्रह).<\/li>\n<li><strong>20 Baisakh 2037 (2 May 1980):<\/strong> voters chose between a <strong>reformed Panchayat system<\/strong> and a <strong>multi-party system<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Result:<\/strong> reformed Panchayat about <strong>54.8%<\/strong>, multi-party about <strong>45.2%<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>third amendment (2037 BS)<\/strong> then brought direct election of the Rastriya Panchayat by adult franchise, and a Prime Minister chosen by that house.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The end of the system<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>16 Poush 2033 (30 December 1976):<\/strong> B.P. Koirala returned from exile in India with a policy of <strong>national reconciliation<\/strong> (राष्ट्रिय मेलमिलाप). He died on 6 Shrawan 2039 (21 July 1982).<\/li>\n<li><strong>2042 BS:<\/strong> the Nepali Congress launched a civil disobedience campaign (<em>satyagraha<\/em>).<\/li>\n<li><strong>2045–2046 BS (1989):<\/strong> trade and transit treaties with India lapsed, and most border points were closed for over a year. Shortages added to public anger.<\/li>\n<li><strong>7 Falgun 2046 (18 February 1990):<\/strong> the People's Movement began (topic 2.8).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h72\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Timeline of the Panchayat period<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date (BS)<\/th>\n<th>Date (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Poush 2017<\/td>\n<td>15 December 1960<\/td>\n<td>Royal takeover<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Baisakh 2018<\/td>\n<td>13 April 1961<\/td>\n<td>14 zones and 75 districts<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Poush 2019<\/td>\n<td>16 December 1962<\/td>\n<td>Constitution of Nepal, 2019<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Bhadra 2020<\/td>\n<td>17 August 1963<\/td>\n<td>Muluki Ain, 2020 in force<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2021<\/td>\n<td>1964<\/td>\n<td>Land Reform Act<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>17 Magh 2028<\/td>\n<td>31 January 1972<\/td>\n<td>Death of King Mahendra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>12 Falgun 2031<\/td>\n<td>24 February 1975<\/td>\n<td>Coronation of King Birendra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10 Jestha 2036<\/td>\n<td>24 May 1979<\/td>\n<td>Referendum announced<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>20 Baisakh 2037<\/td>\n<td>2 May 1980<\/td>\n<td>Referendum held<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>26 Chaitra 2046<\/td>\n<td>8 April 1990<\/td>\n<td>Ban on parties lifted; system ends<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Amendments of the 2019 Constitution<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Amendment<\/th>\n<th>Year (BS)<\/th>\n<th>Main change<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>First<\/td>\n<td>2023<\/td>\n<td>Structure of the system adjusted<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Second<\/td>\n<td>2032<\/td>\n<td>Back-to-the-Village campaign given a constitutional role<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Third<\/td>\n<td>2037<\/td>\n<td>After the referendum: direct election by adult franchise; Prime Minister chosen by the Rastriya Panchayat<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h73\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Length of the Panchayat system<\/td>\n<td>about 30 years (2017–2046 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constitution of 2019<\/td>\n<td>1 Poush 2019<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zones / districts<\/td>\n<td>14 / 75 (2018 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Development regions<\/td>\n<td>4 (2029 BS); 5 (2037 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Muluki Ain<\/td>\n<td>2020 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Zone of Peace proposal<\/td>\n<td>2031 BS; supported by 116 countries<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Referendum<\/td>\n<td>20 Baisakh 2037 (2 May 1980)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Referendum result<\/td>\n<td>about 54.8% to 45.2%<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amendments of the 2019 Constitution<\/td>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h74\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;17 – 18 – 19 – 20 – 21&quot;:<\/strong> takeover (2017), zones and districts (2018), constitution (2019), Muluki Ain (2020), land reform (2021).<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;36 announced, 37 held&quot;:<\/strong> the referendum.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;55 to 45&quot;:<\/strong> the rounded referendum result.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Three amendments: &quot;23 – 32 – 37&quot;.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;Mahendra made it, Birendra reformed it, the people ended it.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h75\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>The referendum was won by the reformed Panchayat side<\/strong>, not the multi-party side.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Two Muluki Ains:<\/strong> 1910 BS (Jung Bahadur) and 2020 BS (King Mahendra). A third law, the Muluki Civil and Criminal Codes, replaced the 2020 code in 2075 BS.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Zones and districts (2018 BS)<\/strong> came before the Constitution of 2019.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Zone of Peace<\/strong> proposal was made by <strong>King Birendra<\/strong>, not King Mahendra.<\/li>\n<li><strong>SAARC's secretariat<\/strong> is in Kathmandu, but SAARC was founded at <strong>Dhaka<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>&quot;Panchayat&quot; in this period means the <strong>party-less political system<\/strong>, not a village council only.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h76\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Points from this period<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td><strong>Jhapa movement<\/strong> of 2028 BS, an early communist uprising; <strong>Chhintang<\/strong> (Dhankuta) incident of 2036 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>In Magh 2018 a bomb was thrown at King Mahendra's vehicle in <strong>Janakpur<\/strong>; <strong>Durgananda Jha<\/strong> was executed for it in 2020 BS and is honoured as a martyr. The Mahendra Highway opened the Terai to settlement.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Seat of the Rastriya Panchayat; students' movement of 2036 BS; coronation of 2031 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td><strong>Pokhara<\/strong> became the centre of the Western Development Region; Prithvi Highway linked it to Kathmandu<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Planned development of <strong>Lumbini<\/strong> began after the visit of United Nations Secretary-General U Thant in 1967; <strong>Butwal<\/strong> grew as a road junction<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td><strong>Birendranagar<\/strong> (Surkhet) was built as a planned town and became the centre of the Mid-Western Development Region<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>The <strong>Far-Western Development Region<\/strong> was created in 2037 BS with its centre at Dipayal<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h77\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.7.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In which year was the national referendum held?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2036 BS<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2037 BS<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2038 BS<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2046 BS<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — It was announced on 10 Jestha 2036 and held on 20 Baisakh 2037 (2 May 1980).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.7.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The Constitution of 2019 set up a party-less Panchayat system.<\/li>\n<li>In the referendum of 2037 BS the multi-party side won.<\/li>\n<li>The Back-to-the-Village campaign belongs to the Panchayat period.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1 and 3 only<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — The reformed Panchayat side won the referendum with about 54.8% of the votes.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.7.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Fill in the blank. King Birendra proposed that Nepal be declared a Zone of Peace in ______ BS.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2028<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2031<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2036<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2042<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — He made the proposal at the time of his coronation in Falgun 2031 (February 1975).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.7.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the measure with its year.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Measure<\/th>\n<th>Year (BS)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Muluki Ain<\/td>\n<td>1. 2028<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Land Reform Act<\/td>\n<td>2. 2037<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. New Education System Plan<\/td>\n<td>3. 2020<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Third amendment of the 2019 Constitution<\/td>\n<td>4. 2021<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-4, B-3, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Muluki Ain 2020, Land Reform Act 2021, New Education System Plan 2028, third amendment 2037.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.7.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In which year was Nepal divided into 14 zones and 75 districts?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2017 BS<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2019 BS<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2018 BS<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2020 BS<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — On 1 Baisakh 2018 (13 April 1961).<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h78\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> State the main features of the Panchayat system and the reasons for its fall.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> <strong>Features:<\/strong> (1) sovereignty and final power lay with the King; (2) political parties were banned and &quot;class organisations&quot; took their place; (3) councils called panchayats worked at village, district and national levels, with the Rastriya Panchayat as the legislature; (4) the system stressed nationalism, one language and one dress, and planned development through zones, districts and development regions; (5) fundamental rights were limited and the press was controlled. <strong>Reasons for its fall:<\/strong> (1) the people had no real share in power, and the banned parties kept working underground; (2) corruption and the privileges of a small circle caused anger; (3) the referendum of 2037 BS showed that nearly half the voters wanted a multi-party system; (4) educated youth and a growing middle class demanded rights; (5) the closing of border trade points in 2045–2046 BS caused shortages; (6) democratic changes abroad encouraged the movement. The Nepali Congress and the United Left Front joined hands, and the People's Movement of 2046 ended the system.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h79\">2.8 From the People's Movement of 2046 to the peace of 2063 (२०४६ देखि २०६३ सम्म)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h80\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Two people's movements, a ten-year armed conflict, a palace massacre and a peace accord fall in these seventeen years. The dates are asked directly, and the order of events is a standard Level II question.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h81\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>The <strong>People's Movement of 2046<\/strong> (जनआन्दोलन) brought back political parties and made the King a constitutional monarch under the Constitution of 2047. Elected governments followed, but they were short-lived. In 2052 BS the Maoists began an armed revolt. In 2058 BS King Birendra and his family were killed in the palace, and King Gyanendra came to the throne. He took power into his own hands in 2059 and 2061 BS. The parliamentary parties and the Maoists then joined against him. The <strong>second People's Movement, in 2062/63<\/strong>, forced him to give up power, and the <strong>Comprehensive Peace Accord<\/strong> ended the war.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h82\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>The People's Movement of 2046<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Began on <strong>7 Falgun 2046 (18 February 1990)<\/strong>, called jointly by the <strong>Nepali Congress<\/strong> and the <strong>United Left Front<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Ganesh Man Singh<\/strong> was its supreme leader (सर्वोच्च कमाण्डर). <strong>Sahana Pradhan<\/strong> chaired the United Left Front.<\/li>\n<li>Late on <strong>26 Chaitra 2046 (8 April 1990)<\/strong> King Birendra lifted the ban on parties.<\/li>\n<li><strong>6 Baisakh 2047 (19 April 1990):<\/strong> an interim government was formed under <strong>Krishna Prasad Bhattarai<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>23 Kartik 2047 (9 November 1990):<\/strong> the <strong>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal, 2047<\/strong> — the fifth constitution. It was drafted by a commission chaired by Justice <strong>Bishwanath Upadhyaya<\/strong>. Features: sovereignty in the people, constitutional monarchy, multi-party parliamentary system, fundamental rights.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Elected governments<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Election of 2048<\/strong> (29 Baisakh 2048; 12 May 1991): 205 seats; Nepali Congress <strong>110<\/strong>, CPN (UML) <strong>69<\/strong>. <strong>Girija Prasad Koirala<\/strong> became Prime Minister.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Mid-term election of 2051<\/strong> (29 Kartik 2051; 15 November 1994): CPN (UML) <strong>88<\/strong>, Nepali Congress <strong>83<\/strong>. <strong>Man Mohan Adhikari<\/strong> led a minority government — the <strong>first communist Prime Minister<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Election of 2056<\/strong> (May 1999): Nepali Congress <strong>111<\/strong>, CPN (UML) <strong>71<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>From 2051 to 2059 BS governments changed almost every year.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The armed conflict<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>The Communist Party of Nepal (Maoist) sent the government a list of <strong>40 demands<\/strong> in Magh 2052.<\/li>\n<li><strong>1 Falgun 2052 (13 February 1996):<\/strong> the Maoists began their &quot;People's War&quot; with attacks in <strong>Rolpa, Rukum, Sindhuli and Gorkha<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The conflict lasted <strong>ten years<\/strong>. About <strong>17,000<\/strong> people were killed.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The palace massacre and the royal takeover<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>19 Jestha 2058 (1 June 2001):<\/strong> King <strong>Birendra<\/strong>, Queen Aishwarya and other members of the royal family were killed at <strong>Narayanhiti Palace<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Crown Prince <strong>Dipendra<\/strong>, lying unconscious, was king for three days. <strong>Gyanendra<\/strong> became king on <strong>22 Jestha 2058 (4 June 2001)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>11 Mangsir 2058 (26 November 2001):<\/strong> a state of emergency was declared and the army was sent against the Maoists.<\/li>\n<li><strong>8 Jestha 2059 (22 May 2002):<\/strong> the House of Representatives was dissolved.<\/li>\n<li><strong>18 Ashoj 2059 (4 October 2002):<\/strong> King Gyanendra dismissed Prime Minister <strong>Sher Bahadur Deuba<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>19 Magh 2061 (1 February 2005):<\/strong> the King took full power himself.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The second People's Movement and peace<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>7 Mangsir 2062 (22 November 2005):<\/strong> the <strong>12-point understanding<\/strong> between the <strong>Seven-Party Alliance<\/strong> and the Maoists, reached in New Delhi.<\/li>\n<li><strong>People's Movement II: 24 Chaitra 2062 – 11 Baisakh 2063 (6–24 April 2006)<\/strong> — 19 days.<\/li>\n<li><strong>11 Baisakh 2063 (24 April 2006):<\/strong> the King restored the dissolved House of Representatives. This is <strong>Loktantra Day<\/strong> (लोकतन्त्र दिवस). Girija Prasad Koirala became Prime Minister.<\/li>\n<li><strong>4 Jestha 2063 (18 May 2006):<\/strong> the House declared itself supreme, cut the King's powers, put the army under the government and declared Nepal a <strong>secular state<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>5 Mangsir 2063 (21 November 2006):<\/strong> the <strong>Comprehensive Peace Accord<\/strong> (विस्तृत शान्ति सम्झौता), signed by Prime Minister <strong>Girija Prasad Koirala<\/strong> and Maoist chairman <strong>Pushpa Kamal Dahal &quot;Prachanda&quot;<\/strong>. The war was formally over.<\/li>\n<li><strong>1 Magh 2063 (15 January 2007):<\/strong> the <strong>Interim Constitution of Nepal, 2063<\/strong> — the sixth constitution. An interim parliament of 330 members, with the Maoists in it, replaced the old House.<\/li>\n<li>The United Nations Mission in Nepal watched over the arms and armies of both sides from 2007 to 2011.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Madhesh movement, Magh 2063 (January–February 2007):<\/strong> protests in the Terai demanded federalism and fair representation. The Interim Constitution was amended to promise a federal system.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h83\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Timeline, 2046–2063 BS<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date (BS)<\/th>\n<th>Date (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>7 Falgun 2046<\/td>\n<td>18 February 1990<\/td>\n<td>People's Movement I begins<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>26 Chaitra 2046<\/td>\n<td>8 April 1990<\/td>\n<td>Ban on parties lifted<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>23 Kartik 2047<\/td>\n<td>9 November 1990<\/td>\n<td>Constitution of 2047<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29 Baisakh 2048<\/td>\n<td>12 May 1991<\/td>\n<td>General election<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>29 Kartik 2051<\/td>\n<td>15 November 1994<\/td>\n<td>Mid-term election<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Falgun 2052<\/td>\n<td>13 February 1996<\/td>\n<td>Armed conflict begins<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19 Jestha 2058<\/td>\n<td>1 June 2001<\/td>\n<td>Palace massacre<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>18 Ashoj 2059<\/td>\n<td>4 October 2002<\/td>\n<td>Elected Prime Minister dismissed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>19 Magh 2061<\/td>\n<td>1 February 2005<\/td>\n<td>King takes full power<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7 Mangsir 2062<\/td>\n<td>22 November 2005<\/td>\n<td>12-point understanding<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>24 Chaitra 2062<\/td>\n<td>6 April 2006<\/td>\n<td>People's Movement II begins<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>11 Baisakh 2063<\/td>\n<td>24 April 2006<\/td>\n<td>House of Representatives restored<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5 Mangsir 2063<\/td>\n<td>21 November 2006<\/td>\n<td>Comprehensive Peace Accord<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1 Magh 2063<\/td>\n<td>15 January 2007<\/td>\n<td>Interim Constitution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>The two People's Movements compared<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Point<\/th>\n<th>Movement I (2046)<\/th>\n<th>Movement II (2062/63)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dates<\/td>\n<td>7 Falgun – 26 Chaitra 2046<\/td>\n<td>24 Chaitra 2062 – 11 Baisakh 2063<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Led by<\/td>\n<td>Nepali Congress and United Left Front<\/td>\n<td>Seven-Party Alliance, with the Maoists in support<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Against<\/td>\n<td>Party-less Panchayat system<\/td>\n<td>Direct rule of King Gyanendra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>King<\/td>\n<td>Birendra<\/td>\n<td>Gyanendra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Result<\/td>\n<td>Multi-party system; constitutional monarchy<\/td>\n<td>Restored parliament; peace process; road to a republic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constitution that followed<\/td>\n<td>2047<\/td>\n<td>Interim Constitution, 2063<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Day observed<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>Loktantra Day, Baisakh 11<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h84\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Seats in the House of Representatives, 2048–2059 BS<\/td>\n<td>205<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepali Congress seats: 2048 / 2051 / 2056<\/td>\n<td>110 / 83 / 111<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CPN (UML) seats: 2048 / 2051 / 2056<\/td>\n<td>69 / 88 / 71<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Maoist demands<\/td>\n<td>40<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Length of the armed conflict<\/td>\n<td>10 years (2052–2063 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Deaths in the conflict<\/td>\n<td>about 17,000<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>People's Movement II<\/td>\n<td>19 days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Points in the understanding of 2062<\/td>\n<td>12<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Interim parliament<\/td>\n<td>330 members<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h85\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;46 – 47 – 48&quot;:<\/strong> movement, constitution, election.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;52 – 58 – 61 – 62 – 63&quot;:<\/strong> war begins, palace massacre, royal takeover, 12 points, peace.<\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;12 points, 19 days, 5 Mangsir&quot;:<\/strong> the understanding, the movement, the peace accord.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Days in order of the calendar:<\/strong> Baisakh 11 (Loktantra) → Jestha 15 (Republic) → Ashoj 3 (Constitution) → Falgun 7 (Democracy).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h86\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Baisakh 11 is Loktantra Day<\/strong>; <strong>Falgun 7 is Prajatantra (Democracy) Day<\/strong>. Both words are translated &quot;democracy&quot; in English.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>12-point understanding (2062)<\/strong> came before the movement; the <strong>Comprehensive Peace Accord (2063)<\/strong> came after it.<\/li>\n<li>The peace accord was signed by <strong>Girija Prasad Koirala and Prachanda<\/strong>, not by the King.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Nepal became secular on 4 Jestha 2063<\/strong>, by declaration of the House — before the republic.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>first communist Prime Minister<\/strong> was Man Mohan Adhikari (2051 BS), not a Maoist leader.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Dipendra was king<\/strong> for three days, although unconscious.<\/li>\n<li>The House of Representatives had <strong>205<\/strong> seats under the 2047 Constitution; today it has 275.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h87\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Points from this period<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Home region of Girija Prasad Koirala and Man Mohan Adhikari (Biratnagar)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td><strong>Madhesh movement of Magh 2063<\/strong>: it began after the death of Ramesh Mahato at <strong>Lahan<\/strong> (Siraha) and spread across the Terai; it put federalism into the Interim Constitution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Narayanhiti Palace; the Ring Road protests of Chaitra 2062 – Baisakh 2063; <strong>Sindhuli<\/strong> was one of the four places attacked on 1 Falgun 2052<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td><strong>Gorkha<\/strong> was one of the four places attacked on 1 Falgun 2052<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td><strong>Rolpa<\/strong> was the heartland of the Maoist movement; <strong>Rukum East<\/strong> now lies in this province<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td><strong>Rukum West<\/strong>, Jajarkot, Kalikot and Jumla were heavily affected by the conflict<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Achham and other hill districts saw major attacks, among them Mangalsen in Falgun 2058<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h88\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.8.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>On which date was the Comprehensive Peace Accord signed?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 11 Baisakh 2063<\/li>\n<li>(b) 7 Mangsir 2062<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 Magh 2063<\/li>\n<li>(d) 5 Mangsir 2063<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — 5 Mangsir 2063 (21 November 2006). 7 Mangsir 2062 is the date of the 12-point understanding.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.8.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the events in order of time, earliest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Palace massacre<\/li>\n<li>Start of the Maoist armed conflict<\/li>\n<li>12-point understanding<\/li>\n<li>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal, 2047<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 4 – 1 – 2 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(c) 4 – 2 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 4 – 2 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — 2047, 2052, 2058, 2062.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.8.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the person with the role.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Person<\/th>\n<th>Role<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Ganesh Man Singh<\/td>\n<td>1. First communist Prime Minister<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Krishna Prasad Bhattarai<\/td>\n<td>2. Chair of the commission that drafted the 2047 Constitution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Bishwanath Upadhyaya<\/td>\n<td>3. Supreme leader of the People's Movement of 2046<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Man Mohan Adhikari<\/td>\n<td>4. Prime Minister of the interim government of 2047<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Ganesh Man Singh led the movement, K.P. Bhattarai headed the interim government, Bishwanath Upadhyaya chaired the drafting commission, and Man Mohan Adhikari was the first communist Prime Minister.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.8.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Assertion (A):<\/strong> The People's Movement of 2062/63 ended on 11 Baisakh 2063.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reason (R):<\/strong> On that day the King restored the dissolved House of Representatives.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Both A and R are true, and R explains A.<\/li>\n<li>(b) Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A.<\/li>\n<li>(c) A is true, but R is false.<\/li>\n<li>(d) A is false, but R is true.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — The restoration of the House met the main demand of the Seven-Party Alliance, so the movement was called off.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.8.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>On which date did the Maoist armed conflict begin?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 Falgun 2052<\/li>\n<li>(b) 7 Falgun 2046<\/li>\n<li>(c) 19 Jestha 2058<\/li>\n<li>(d) 19 Magh 2061<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — 1 Falgun 2052 (13 February 1996).<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h89\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> Compare the People's Movements of 2046 and 2062/63.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> <strong>Similarities:<\/strong> both were peaceful mass movements led by an alliance of parties; both were directed against the active rule of the King; both ended when the King gave way after weeks of protest; both were followed by a new constitution. <strong>Differences:<\/strong> (1) <em>Target<\/em> — the first opposed the party-less Panchayat system; the second opposed the direct rule of King Gyanendra. (2) <em>Leadership<\/em> — the first was led by the Nepali Congress and the United Left Front; the second by the Seven-Party Alliance, supported by the Maoists under the 12-point understanding. (3) <em>Length<\/em> — the first lasted about seven weeks, the second 19 days. (4) <em>Aim and result<\/em> — the first accepted a constitutional monarchy and brought the Constitution of 2047; the second led to the peace accord, the Interim Constitution of 2063, a secular state and finally a republic. (5) <em>Reach<\/em> — the second was wider, with rural people, women, professionals and civil society taking part in great numbers. In short, the first movement limited the monarchy; the second opened the way to end it.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h90\">2.9 Republic, Constitution and events to the present (गणतन्त्र, संविधान र हालसम्मका घटना)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h91\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>This is where history meets current affairs. The dates of the republic and the Constitution, the &quot;firsts&quot; among office-holders, and the latest change of government are all likely questions. Facts after 2079 BS are marked 🔄 because they are recent.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h92\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>After the peace accord, the people elected a <strong>Constituent Assembly<\/strong> to write a new constitution. Its first meeting ended the monarchy and made Nepal a <strong>republic<\/strong>. The first Assembly could not finish its work; a second one was elected and delivered the <strong>Constitution of Nepal<\/strong> in 2072 BS. The Constitution made Nepal a <strong>federal democratic republic<\/strong> with seven provinces. Since then there have been three general elections, many changes of government, a youth-led protest movement in 2082 BS, and an early election in which a new party won a majority.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h93\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<p><strong>The republic<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>First Constituent Assembly election: 28 Chaitra 2064 (10 April 2008).<\/strong> 601 members. The Communist Party of Nepal (Maoist) was the largest party.<\/li>\n<li><strong>15 Jestha 2065 (28 May 2008):<\/strong> the first meeting of the Assembly declared Nepal a <strong>Federal Democratic Republic<\/strong>. The vote was <strong>560 to 4<\/strong>. The monarchy of about 240 years ended. <strong>Republic Day<\/strong> is Jestha 15.<\/li>\n<li><strong>First President: Dr Ram Baran Yadav<\/strong>, elected on 6 Shrawan 2065 (21 July 2008). <strong>First Vice-President: Parmananda Jha.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>First Prime Minister of the republic: Pushpa Kamal Dahal &quot;Prachanda&quot;<\/strong>, elected on 31 Shrawan 2065 (15 August 2008).<\/li>\n<li>The first Assembly's term was extended several times. It ended on <strong>14 Jestha 2069 (27 May 2012)<\/strong> without a constitution.<\/li>\n<li><strong>1 Chaitra 2069 (14 March 2013):<\/strong> Chief Justice <strong>Khil Raj Regmi<\/strong> became chairman of an interim council of ministers to hold a new election.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The Constitution<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Second Constituent Assembly election: 4 Mangsir 2070 (19 November 2013).<\/strong> Nepali Congress <strong>196<\/strong>, CPN (UML) <strong>175<\/strong>, Unified CPN (Maoist) <strong>80<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The earthquake of 12 Baisakh 2072 pushed the parties to agree. The <strong>16-point agreement<\/strong> of 25 Jestha 2072 (8 June 2015) settled the main disputes.<\/li>\n<li><strong>30 Bhadra 2072 (16 September 2015):<\/strong> the Assembly passed the Constitution — <strong>507 votes for, 25 against<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>3 Ashoj 2072 (20 September 2015):<\/strong> President <strong>Ram Baran Yadav<\/strong> proclaimed the <strong>Constitution of Nepal<\/strong>, the seventh constitution. It has <strong>35 Parts, 308 Articles and 9 Schedules<\/strong>. <strong>Constitution Day<\/strong> (National Day) is Ashoj 3.<\/li>\n<li>Protests in the Terai and a blockade of border points followed in late 2072.<\/li>\n<li><strong>First amendment:<\/strong> authenticated on 16 Falgun 2072 (28 February 2016). <strong>Second amendment:<\/strong> 4 Ashad 2077 (18 June 2020), for the new map in the coat of arms.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Putting federalism to work<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Presidents:<\/strong> Ram Baran Yadav (2065) → <strong>Bidya Devi Bhandari<\/strong>, elected 11 Kartik 2072 (28 October 2015), the <strong>first woman President<\/strong> → <strong>Ram Chandra Paudel<\/strong>, elected 25 Falgun 2079 (9 March 2023).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Onsari Gharti Magar<\/strong> became the first woman Speaker in 2072. <strong>Sushila Karki<\/strong> became the first woman Chief Justice on 27 Ashad 2073 (11 July 2016).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Local elections of 2074<\/strong> — the first in twenty years — were held in three phases: 31 Baisakh, 14 Ashad and 2 Ashoj 2074.<\/li>\n<li><strong>First federal and provincial elections under the Constitution:<\/strong> 10 and 21 Mangsir 2074 (26 November and 7 December 2017). <strong>K.P. Sharma Oli<\/strong> became Prime Minister on 3 Falgun 2074.<\/li>\n<li>The House of Representatives was dissolved on <strong>5 Poush 2077<\/strong> and again on <strong>8 Jestha 2078<\/strong>. The <strong>Supreme Court restored it both times<\/strong> (11 Falgun 2077; 28 Ashad 2078). <strong>Sher Bahadur Deuba<\/strong> became Prime Minister on 29 Ashad 2078.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Elections of 2079:<\/strong> local on 30 Baisakh 2079 (13 May 2022); federal and provincial on <strong>4 Mangsir 2079 (20 November 2022)<\/strong>. Nepali Congress 89, CPN (UML) 78, CPN (Maoist Centre) 32, Rastriya Swatantra Party 20. <strong>Pushpa Kamal Dahal<\/strong> became Prime Minister in Poush 2079.<\/li>\n<li><strong>31 Ashad 2081 (15 July 2024):<\/strong> K.P. Sharma Oli became Prime Minister, leading a coalition of the CPN (UML) and the Nepali Congress.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>The movement of 2082 BS and the early election<\/strong> 🔄<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>In Bhadra 2082 the government ordered many social-media platforms to be blocked. Young people, already angry about corruption, called protests. The movement is known as the <strong>Gen Z movement<\/strong> (जेनजी आन्दोलन).<\/li>\n<li><strong>23 Bhadra 2082 (8 September 2025):<\/strong> police fired on protesters in Kathmandu; 19 people were reported killed that day. <strong>24 Bhadra (9 September):<\/strong> Prime Minister <strong>K.P. Sharma Oli resigned<\/strong>. The parliament building, Singha Durbar and other public buildings were set on fire.<\/li>\n<li>⚖️ The number of dead in the movement is given as <strong>72<\/strong> in the Home Ministry's count of that month and as <strong>76<\/strong> in later counts.<\/li>\n<li><strong>27 Bhadra 2082 (12 September 2025):<\/strong> former Chief Justice <strong>Sushila Karki<\/strong> became interim Prime Minister — the <strong>first woman Prime Minister of Nepal<\/strong>. The House of Representatives was dissolved, and an election was called.<\/li>\n<li><strong>21 Falgun 2082 (5 March 2026):<\/strong> election to the House of Representatives. The <strong>Rastriya Swatantra Party won 182 of the 275 seats<\/strong> — the first single-party majority since 2056 BS.<\/li>\n<li><strong>13 Chaitra 2082 (27 March 2026):<\/strong> <strong>Balendra Shah<\/strong>, former Mayor of Kathmandu, became Prime Minister.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h94\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Timeline, 2064 BS to the present<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Date (BS)<\/th>\n<th>Date (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Event<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>28 Chaitra 2064<\/td>\n<td>10 April 2008<\/td>\n<td>First Constituent Assembly election<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>15 Jestha 2065<\/td>\n<td>28 May 2008<\/td>\n<td>Republic declared<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>14 Jestha 2069<\/td>\n<td>27 May 2012<\/td>\n<td>First Constituent Assembly ends<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 Mangsir 2070<\/td>\n<td>19 November 2013<\/td>\n<td>Second Constituent Assembly election<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3 Ashoj 2072<\/td>\n<td>20 September 2015<\/td>\n<td>Constitution of Nepal proclaimed<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>10 and 21 Mangsir 2074<\/td>\n<td>26 November and 7 December 2017<\/td>\n<td>First elections under the Constitution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 Ashad 2077<\/td>\n<td>18 June 2020<\/td>\n<td>Second amendment<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4 Mangsir 2079<\/td>\n<td>20 November 2022<\/td>\n<td>General election<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>23–24 Bhadra 2082<\/td>\n<td>8–9 September 2025<\/td>\n<td>Gen Z movement; Prime Minister resigns<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>27 Bhadra 2082<\/td>\n<td>12 September 2025<\/td>\n<td>Sushila Karki interim Prime Minister<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>21 Falgun 2082<\/td>\n<td>5 March 2026<\/td>\n<td>Early general election<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>13 Chaitra 2082<\/td>\n<td>27 March 2026<\/td>\n<td>Balendra Shah Prime Minister 🔄<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>House of Representatives, election of 21 Falgun 2082<\/strong> 🔄<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Party<\/th>\n<th>Seats<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Rastriya Swatantra Party<\/td>\n<td>182<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepali Congress<\/td>\n<td>38<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>CPN (UML)<\/td>\n<td>25<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Nepali Communist Party<\/td>\n<td>17<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Shram Sanskriti Party<\/td>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rastriya Prajatantra Party<\/td>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Others<\/td>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td><strong>Total<\/strong><\/td>\n<td><strong>275<\/strong><\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p>The Rastriya Swatantra Party's 182 seats are 125 directly elected and 57 proportional. These figures come from published election results; confirm them with the Election Commission before your examination.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Heads of state of the republic<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>President<\/th>\n<th>Elected<\/th>\n<th>Note<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Dr Ram Baran Yadav<\/td>\n<td>6 Shrawan 2065 (21 July 2008)<\/td>\n<td>First President; proclaimed the Constitution<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bidya Devi Bhandari<\/td>\n<td>11 Kartik 2072 (28 October 2015)<\/td>\n<td>First woman President; served two terms<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ram Chandra Paudel<\/td>\n<td>25 Falgun 2079 (9 March 2023)<\/td>\n<td>Third President<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h95\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Members of the Constituent Assembly<\/td>\n<td>601<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vote for the republic<\/td>\n<td>560 to 4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Republic Day<\/td>\n<td>Jestha 15<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Vote on the Constitution<\/td>\n<td>507 for, 25 against<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constitution Day<\/td>\n<td>Ashoj 3<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constitution<\/td>\n<td>35 Parts, 308 Articles, 9 Schedules<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Amendments so far<\/td>\n<td>2 (2072 and 2077 BS)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Members of the House of Representatives<\/td>\n<td>275 (165 directly elected + 110 proportional)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Election of 2082: largest party 🔄<\/td>\n<td>Rastriya Swatantra Party, 182 seats<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h96\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>&quot;64 – 65 – 70 – 72&quot;:<\/strong> first election, republic, second election, Constitution.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Two Assemblies: &quot;the first made the republic, the second made the Constitution.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;507 for, 25 against; 35 – 308 – 9.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Sushila Karki, twice first:<\/strong> first woman Chief Justice (2073), first woman Prime Minister (2082).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Presidents: Y – B – P<\/strong> (Yadav, Bhandari, Paudel).<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h97\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>The republic was <strong>declared on 15 Jestha 2065<\/strong>, not on the day of the Constitution.<\/li>\n<li>The Constitution was <strong>proclaimed by President Ram Baran Yadav<\/strong>. Bidya Devi Bhandari became President a month later.<\/li>\n<li>The Constitution was written by the <strong>second<\/strong> Constituent Assembly.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Passed on 30 Bhadra; proclaimed on 3 Ashoj.<\/strong> Two different dates.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>first President<\/strong> (Ram Baran Yadav) and the <strong>first Prime Minister of the republic<\/strong> (Prachanda) are different persons.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Sushila Karki<\/strong> was appointed in 2082 as an <strong>interim<\/strong> Prime Minister from outside parliament.<\/li>\n<li>Seat numbers of 2079 and 2082 are easily mixed. Learn them with the year 🔄.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h98\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>Points from this period<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Named on 17 Falgun 2079, the last province to be named; in the election of 2082 Balendra Shah defeated K.P. Sharma Oli in a Jhapa constituency 🔄<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>Home province of the first President, Dr Ram Baran Yadav (Dhanusha); protests against parts of the Constitution in 2072; named <strong>Madhesh Province<\/strong> with <strong>Janakpurdham<\/strong> as capital on 3 Magh 2078 (17 January 2022)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Seat of the Constituent Assemblies; the districts worst hit by the 2072 earthquake; main scene of the 2082 movement<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Epicentre of the earthquake at Barpak, Gorkha; capital Pokhara<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Named on 20 Ashoj 2077 with its capital at Deukhuri, Dang<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>The first province to choose its name (Falgun 2074)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Named in Ashoj 2075 with its capital at Godawari, Kailali<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h99\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.9.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>On which date was Nepal declared a republic?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 11 Baisakh 2063<\/li>\n<li>(b) 28 Chaitra 2064<\/li>\n<li>(c) 15 Jestha 2065<\/li>\n<li>(d) 3 Ashoj 2072<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — The first meeting of the Constituent Assembly declared the republic on 15 Jestha 2065 (28 May 2008).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.9.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the person with the &quot;first&quot;.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Person<\/th>\n<th>First<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Ram Baran Yadav<\/td>\n<td>1. First woman Prime Minister<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Bidya Devi Bhandari<\/td>\n<td>2. First Prime Minister of the republic<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Sushila Karki<\/td>\n<td>3. First President<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Pushpa Kamal Dahal<\/td>\n<td>4. First woman President<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-2, B-4, C-1, D-3<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Ram Baran Yadav was the first President, Bidya Devi Bhandari the first woman President, Sushila Karki the first woman Prime Minister, and Pushpa Kamal Dahal the first Prime Minister of the republic.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.9.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>The Constitution of Nepal was proclaimed on 3 Ashoj 2072.<\/li>\n<li>It was proclaimed by President Bidya Devi Bhandari.<\/li>\n<li>It was made by the second Constituent Assembly.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — President Ram Baran Yadav proclaimed the Constitution. Bidya Devi Bhandari was elected President on 11 Kartik 2072.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.9.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the events in order of time, earliest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Second Constituent Assembly election<\/li>\n<li>Declaration of the republic<\/li>\n<li>Proclamation of the Constitution of Nepal<\/li>\n<li>First Constituent Assembly election<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 4 – 1 – 2 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<li>(c) 4 – 2 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(d) 4 – 2 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — 2064, 2065, 2070, 2072.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.9.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which party won a majority in the House of Representatives election of 21 Falgun 2082?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Nepali Congress<\/li>\n<li>(b) Rastriya Swatantra Party<\/li>\n<li>(c) CPN (UML)<\/li>\n<li>(d) Rastriya Prajatantra Party<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — The Rastriya Swatantra Party won 182 of 275 seats. 🔄 Recent fact; confirm it before your examination.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h100\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> Trace the main steps from the Comprehensive Peace Accord of 2063 to the Constitution of 2072.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> (1) <strong>Comprehensive Peace Accord, 5 Mangsir 2063<\/strong> — the government and the Maoists ended the war and agreed to elect a Constituent Assembly. (2) <strong>Interim Constitution, 1 Magh 2063<\/strong> — it brought the Maoists into parliament and government; after the Madhesh movement it promised federalism. (3) <strong>First Constituent Assembly election, 28 Chaitra 2064.<\/strong> (4) <strong>Republic, 15 Jestha 2065<\/strong> — the Assembly's first meeting ended the monarchy; a President and a new government were elected. (5) <strong>Failure of the first Assembly, 14 Jestha 2069<\/strong> — the parties could not agree on the number and names of provinces and the form of government. (6) <strong>Interim election government<\/strong> under Chief Justice Khil Raj Regmi, Chaitra 2069. (7) <strong>Second Constituent Assembly election, 4 Mangsir 2070.<\/strong> (8) <strong>16-point agreement, 25 Jestha 2072<\/strong>, after the earthquake. (9) <strong>Adoption on 30 Bhadra 2072<\/strong> by 507 votes to 25, and <strong>proclamation on 3 Ashoj 2072<\/strong> by President Ram Baran Yadav. The process took nearly nine years, but it turned a country at war into a federal democratic republic by peaceful means.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h101\">2.10 Timeline: treaties, wars, constitutions and firsts (सन्धि, युद्ध, संविधान र पहिला घटना)<\/h2>\n<h4 id=\"h102\">1 · Why it matters<\/h4>\n<p>Level II questions join facts from different periods: &quot;arrange in order&quot;, &quot;match the treaty with the year&quot;, &quot;which one does not belong&quot;. This topic gathers those facts into lists so that you can answer such questions quickly.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h103\">2 · Simple explanation<\/h4>\n<p>History questions repeat four kinds of list: <strong>treaties<\/strong> (with whom, when, what result), <strong>wars<\/strong>, <strong>constitutions<\/strong>, and <strong>&quot;firsts&quot;<\/strong> (the first person or institution of a kind). Learn each list in time order. Then a question that mixes periods becomes a matter of placing each item on one line.<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h104\">3 · Key facts<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li>Nepal has fought <strong>four wars with outside powers<\/strong> since unification: with Tibet (1788–1789), with Tibet and China (1791–1792), with the British East India Company (1814–1816) and with Tibet again (1855–1856).<\/li>\n<li>Nepal has had <strong>seven constitutions<\/strong>: 2004, 2007, 2015, 2019, 2047, 2063 and 2072 BS.<\/li>\n<li>The first country with which Nepal set up diplomatic relations was <strong>Britain (1816)<\/strong>. The second was the <strong>United States (25 April 1947)<\/strong>, then <strong>India (13 June 1947)<\/strong>, <strong>France (1949)<\/strong>, <strong>China (1 August 1955)<\/strong> and <strong>Japan (1956)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Nepal joined the <strong>United Nations on 14 December 1955<\/strong>, was a <strong>founding member of the Non-Aligned Movement (1961)<\/strong> and of <strong>SAARC (1985)<\/strong>, and joined the <strong>World Trade Organization on 23 April 2004<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>National days that come from history:<\/strong> Poush 27 (Prithvi Jayanti, National Unity Day) · Magh 16 (Martyrs' Day) · Falgun 7 (Democracy Day) · Baisakh 11 (Loktantra Day) · Jestha 15 (Republic Day) · Ashoj 3 (Constitution Day).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Kings of the Shah dynasty after unification:<\/strong> Prithvi Narayan → Pratap Singh → Rana Bahadur → Girvan Yuddha → Rajendra → Surendra → Prithvi Bir Bikram → Tribhuvan → Mahendra → Birendra → Dipendra → Gyanendra.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h105\">4 · Tables<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Treaties and agreements<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Year (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Treaty<\/th>\n<th>With<\/th>\n<th>Main point<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1789<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Kerung<\/td>\n<td>Tibet<\/td>\n<td>Ended the first war; Tibet agreed to pay a yearly sum<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1792<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Betrawati<\/td>\n<td>China and Tibet<\/td>\n<td>Ended the second war; Nepal to send a mission to China every five years<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1792<\/td>\n<td>Commercial treaty<\/td>\n<td>East India Company<\/td>\n<td>First treaty with the British<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1815–1816<\/td>\n<td>Sugauli Treaty<\/td>\n<td>East India Company<\/td>\n<td>Mechi–Mahakali boundary; British Resident<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1856<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Thapathali<\/td>\n<td>Tibet<\/td>\n<td>Yearly payment to Nepal; trade rights; Nepali envoy at Lhasa<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1860<\/td>\n<td>Boundary treaty<\/td>\n<td>British India<\/td>\n<td>Naya Muluk returned<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1923<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Friendship<\/td>\n<td>Britain<\/td>\n<td>Britain recognised Nepal's independence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1947<\/td>\n<td>Tripartite agreement<\/td>\n<td>Britain and India<\/td>\n<td>Gorkha recruitment after Indian independence<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1950<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Peace and Friendship<\/td>\n<td>India<\/td>\n<td>Signed 31 July 1950<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1954<\/td>\n<td>Koshi Agreement<\/td>\n<td>India<\/td>\n<td>Koshi barrage and canals<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1959<\/td>\n<td>Gandak Agreement<\/td>\n<td>India<\/td>\n<td>Gandak barrage and canals<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1960<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Peace and Friendship<\/td>\n<td>China<\/td>\n<td>Signed 28 April 1960<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1961<\/td>\n<td>Boundary treaty<\/td>\n<td>China<\/td>\n<td>Signed 5 October 1961<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1996<\/td>\n<td>Mahakali Treaty<\/td>\n<td>India<\/td>\n<td>Sharing of the Mahakali river<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Wars with outside powers<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Years (AD)<\/th>\n<th>War<\/th>\n<th>Ended by<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1788–1789<\/td>\n<td>First war with Tibet<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Kerung<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1791–1792<\/td>\n<td>War with Tibet and China<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Betrawati<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1814–1816<\/td>\n<td>Anglo-Nepal War<\/td>\n<td>Sugauli Treaty<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>1855–1856<\/td>\n<td>War with Tibet<\/td>\n<td>Treaty of Thapathali<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>The seven constitutions<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>No.<\/th>\n<th>Constitution<\/th>\n<th>Date (BS)<\/th>\n<th>Date (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Given by<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>1<\/td>\n<td>Government of Nepal Act, 2004<\/td>\n<td>13 Magh 2004<\/td>\n<td>26 January 1948<\/td>\n<td>Prime Minister Padma Shamsher<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>2<\/td>\n<td>Interim Government of Nepal Act, 2007<\/td>\n<td>Chaitra 2007<\/td>\n<td>1951<\/td>\n<td>King Tribhuvan<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>3<\/td>\n<td>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal, 2015<\/td>\n<td>1 Falgun 2015<\/td>\n<td>12 February 1959<\/td>\n<td>King Mahendra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<td>Constitution of Nepal, 2019<\/td>\n<td>1 Poush 2019<\/td>\n<td>16 December 1962<\/td>\n<td>King Mahendra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>5<\/td>\n<td>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal, 2047<\/td>\n<td>23 Kartik 2047<\/td>\n<td>9 November 1990<\/td>\n<td>King Birendra<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>6<\/td>\n<td>Interim Constitution of Nepal, 2063<\/td>\n<td>1 Magh 2063<\/td>\n<td>15 January 2007<\/td>\n<td>Parliament<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<td>Constitution of Nepal<\/td>\n<td>3 Ashoj 2072<\/td>\n<td>20 September 2015<\/td>\n<td>Constituent Assembly; proclaimed by the President<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Firsts in the history of Nepal<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>First<\/th>\n<th>Name<\/th>\n<th>Year<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>King of unified Nepal<\/td>\n<td>Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/td>\n<td>1768 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prime Minister (as Mukhtiyar)<\/td>\n<td>Bhimsen Thapa<\/td>\n<td>1806 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>To use the title &quot;Prime Minister&quot;<\/td>\n<td>Mathbar Singh Thapa<\/td>\n<td>1843 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rana Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>Jung Bahadur<\/td>\n<td>1846 AD<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Martyr<\/td>\n<td>Lakhan Thapa Magar<\/td>\n<td>1933 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>School<\/td>\n<td>Durbar High School<\/td>\n<td>1910 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Hospital<\/td>\n<td>Bir Hospital<\/td>\n<td>1947 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Newspaper<\/td>\n<td>Gorkhapatra<\/td>\n<td>1958 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Census<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>1968 BS (1911 AD)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>College<\/td>\n<td>Tri-Chandra College<\/td>\n<td>1975 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Political party<\/td>\n<td>Nepal Praja Parishad<\/td>\n<td>1993 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bank<\/td>\n<td>Nepal Bank Limited<\/td>\n<td>1994 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Constitution<\/td>\n<td>Government of Nepal Act<\/td>\n<td>2004 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prime Minister from the people<\/td>\n<td>Matrika Prasad Koirala<\/td>\n<td>2008 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Budget<\/td>\n<td>Presented on 21 Magh<\/td>\n<td>2008 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Five-Year Plan<\/td>\n<td>First Plan<\/td>\n<td>2013 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>General election<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<td>2015 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Elected Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>B.P. Koirala<\/td>\n<td>2016 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Woman minister<\/td>\n<td>Dwarika Devi Thakurani<\/td>\n<td>2016 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>University<\/td>\n<td>Tribhuvan University<\/td>\n<td>2016 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Communist Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>Man Mohan Adhikari<\/td>\n<td>2051 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>President<\/td>\n<td>Dr Ram Baran Yadav<\/td>\n<td>2065 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Woman President<\/td>\n<td>Bidya Devi Bhandari<\/td>\n<td>2072 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Woman Speaker<\/td>\n<td>Onsari Gharti Magar<\/td>\n<td>2072 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Woman Chief Justice<\/td>\n<td>Sushila Karki<\/td>\n<td>2073 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Woman Prime Minister<\/td>\n<td>Sushila Karki<\/td>\n<td>2082 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Shah kings after unification<\/strong><\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>King<\/th>\n<th>Reign (AD)<\/th>\n<th>Note<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/td>\n<td>1768–1775<\/td>\n<td>King of Gorkha from 1743<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Pratap Singh Shah<\/td>\n<td>1775–1777<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rana Bahadur Shah<\/td>\n<td>1777–1799<\/td>\n<td>Regents Rajendra Laxmi and Bahadur Shah<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Girvan Yuddha Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>1799–1816<\/td>\n<td>Anglo-Nepal War; Sugauli Treaty<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Rajendra Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>1816–1847<\/td>\n<td>Kot Massacre in his reign<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Surendra Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>1847–1881<\/td>\n<td>Jung Bahadur's rule<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Prithvi Bir Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>1881–1911<\/td>\n<td>—<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Tribhuvan Bir Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>1911–1955<\/td>\n<td>Revolution of 2007 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Mahendra Bir Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>1955–1972<\/td>\n<td>Takeover of 2017 BS; Panchayat<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Birendra Bir Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>1972–2001<\/td>\n<td>Referendum; movement of 2046 BS<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dipendra Bir Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>1–4 June 2001<\/td>\n<td>Three days<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gyanendra Bir Bikram Shah<\/td>\n<td>2001–2008<\/td>\n<td>Also child king in 1950–1951; last king<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h106\">5 · Numbers and dates to remember<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Fact<\/th>\n<th>Figure<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Constitutions<\/td>\n<td>7<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Wars with outside powers since unification<\/td>\n<td>4<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diplomatic relations with Britain<\/td>\n<td>1816<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diplomatic relations with the United States<\/td>\n<td>25 April 1947<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diplomatic relations with India<\/td>\n<td>13 June 1947<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Diplomatic relations with China<\/td>\n<td>1 August 1955<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>United Nations membership<\/td>\n<td>14 December 1955<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>World Trade Organization membership<\/td>\n<td>23 April 2004<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Era conversions<\/td>\n<td>BS = AD + 57 (or 56 from January to mid-April); Nepal Sambat = AD − 879 or 880; Shaka Sambat = AD − 78 or 79<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h107\">6 · Memory aid<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Constitutions: &quot;04 – 07 – 15 – 19 – 47 – 63 – 72&quot;.<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>Treaties with the north: &quot;K – B – T&quot;<\/strong> — Kerung (1789), Betrawati (1792), Thapathali (1856).<\/li>\n<li><strong>Treaties with the British: &quot;Sugauli takes land (1816), 1860 returns land, 1923 recognises.&quot;<\/strong><\/li>\n<li><strong>&quot;1947 is the year of three&quot;:<\/strong> relations with the United States, relations with India, and the tripartite agreement.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Schools to banks: &quot;10 – 47 – 58 – 75 – 94&quot;<\/strong> — school (1910), hospital (1947), newspaper (1958), college (1975), bank (1994), all BS.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h108\">7 · Exam traps<\/h4>\n<ul>\n<li><strong>Kerung, Betrawati and Thapathali<\/strong> are treaties with Tibet or China. <strong>Sugauli<\/strong> is with the British.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Treaty of 1950<\/strong> is with <strong>India<\/strong>; the <strong>Treaty of 1923<\/strong> is with <strong>Britain<\/strong>; the <strong>Treaty of 1960<\/strong> is with <strong>China<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>1947 has two meanings:<\/strong> 1947 <strong>AD<\/strong> (relations with the United States and India) and 1947 <strong>BS<\/strong> (Bir Hospital). Check the era.<\/li>\n<li>&quot;First Prime Minister&quot; has several answers: Bhimsen Thapa (first of all), Jung Bahadur (first Rana), M.P. Koirala (first from the people), B.P. Koirala (first elected), Prachanda (first of the republic). Read the qualifier.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>interim<\/strong> documents of 2007 and 2063 are counted among the seven constitutions.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Gyanendra<\/strong> appears twice in the list of kings.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>first census<\/strong> (1968 BS) and the <strong>first general election<\/strong> (2015 BS) are often confused in &quot;first&quot; lists.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<h4 id=\"h109\">8 · Province link<\/h4>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Province<\/th>\n<th>An item from the lists<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Koshi<\/td>\n<td>Koshi Agreement (1954); Biratnagar Jute Mill, first modern factory<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Madhesh<\/td>\n<td>Terai lands given up under the Sugauli Treaty and returned in December 1816; canals of the Koshi and Gandak agreements irrigate its fields; the first President came from Dhanusha<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Bagmati<\/td>\n<td>Thapathali (treaty of 1856) and Betrawati (treaty of 1792); Durbar High School, Bir Hospital, Tri-Chandra College, Tribhuvan University<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Gandaki<\/td>\n<td>Gorkha, where the Shah kingdom began (1559); Gandak Agreement (1959) on the Narayani river<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Lumbini<\/td>\n<td>Naya Muluk (1860): Banke and Bardiya<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Karnali<\/td>\n<td>Oldest inscriptions in early Nepali, from Dullu and Sinja<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Sudurpashchim<\/td>\n<td>Mahakali Treaty (1996); Naya Muluk (1860): Kailali and Kanchanpur<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<h4 id=\"h110\">9 · Practice MCQs<\/h4>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.10.1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>With which country did Nepal first set up diplomatic relations?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) India<\/li>\n<li>(b) China<\/li>\n<li>(c) Britain<\/li>\n<li>(d) United States<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Relations with Britain date from 1816. The United States (1947) was second.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.10.2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the treaty with its year.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Treaty<\/th>\n<th>Year (AD)<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Betrawati<\/td>\n<td>1. 1923<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Sugauli (ratified)<\/td>\n<td>2. 1856<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Thapathali<\/td>\n<td>3. 1792<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Friendship treaty with Britain<\/td>\n<td>4. 1816<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Betrawati 1792, Sugauli 1816, Thapathali 1856, treaty with Britain 1923.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.10.3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the constitutions in order of time, earliest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Interim Constitution of Nepal<\/li>\n<li>Government of Nepal Act<\/li>\n<li>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal given by King Birendra<\/li>\n<li>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal given by King Mahendra<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 2 – 4 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 – 3 – 4 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(c) 4 – 2 – 3 – 1<\/li>\n<li>(d) 2 – 4 – 1 – 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — 2004, 2015, 2047, 2063.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.10.4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which one does not belong to the group?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Treaty of Kerung<\/li>\n<li>(b) Sugauli Treaty<\/li>\n<li>(c) Treaty of Betrawati<\/li>\n<li>(d) Treaty of Thapathali<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — The Sugauli Treaty was made with the British East India Company. The other three followed wars with Tibet or China.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>Q 2.10.5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In which year did Nepal become a member of the United Nations?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1947 AD<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1950 AD<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1961 AD<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1955 AD<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — 14 December 1955 (29 Mangsir 2012 BS).<\/p>\n<h4 id=\"h111\">10 · Short-answer question<\/h4>\n<p><strong>Question.<\/strong> List the seven constitutions of Nepal and state one main feature of each.<\/p>\n<p class=\"ans model\"><strong>Model answer.<\/strong> (1) <strong>Government of Nepal Act, 2004<\/strong> — announced by Padma Shamsher; the first written constitution, with a limited legislature under Rana control; it never came into real force. (2) <strong>Interim Government of Nepal Act, 2007<\/strong> — issued after the revolution; it gave executive power to the King and his council of ministers until an elected body could make a constitution. (3) <strong>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal, 2015<\/strong> — given by King Mahendra; a parliamentary system with two houses, under which the first general election was held. (4) <strong>Constitution of Nepal, 2019<\/strong> — the party-less Panchayat system, with sovereignty in the King. (5) <strong>Constitution of the Kingdom of Nepal, 2047<\/strong> — after the People's Movement; sovereignty in the people, constitutional monarchy and multi-party democracy. (6) <strong>Interim Constitution of Nepal, 2063<\/strong> — after the second People's Movement; it managed the peace process and provided for a Constituent Assembly, a secular state and, by amendment, a federal republic. (7) <strong>Constitution of Nepal (2072)<\/strong> — written by the elected Constituent Assembly; a federal democratic republic with three levels of government and seven provinces.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h2 id=\"h112\">Chapter 2 — End matter<\/h2>\n<h3 id=\"h113\">A. Quick Revision<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Ancient period<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Dynasties in order: Gopal (8 kings) → Mahispal (3) → Kirat (29 or 32; Yalambar first, Gasti last) → Soma (5) → Lichhavi.<\/li>\n<li>Oldest inscription in Nepal: Ashoka pillar, Lumbini, 249 BC, Brahmi script. Oldest dated inscription of the Valley: Changu Narayan, 464 AD.<\/li>\n<li>Buddha: born at Lumbini (563 BC by textbooks; 623 BC in the World Heritage listing). Lumbini pillar rediscovered 1896; World Heritage Site 1997.<\/li>\n<li>Janakpur: capital of Videha. Tilaurakot: capital of the Shakyas.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Lichhavi period<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Manadeva: Changu Narayan inscription, Mananka, Managriha. Amshuverma: Kailashkut Bhawan, <em>Shabda Vidya<\/em>. Narendradeva: Bhadradhivas.<\/li>\n<li>Taxes: bhaga (land), bhoga (animals), kara (trade). Council: Panchali.<\/li>\n<li>Nepal Sambat: 879 AD, Raghavadeva; Shankhadhar Sakhwa.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Medieval period<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Jayasthiti Malla (1382–1395): castes, weights and measures, land grades. Yaksha Malla died 1482; three kingdoms.<\/li>\n<li>Last kings: Jaya Prakash (Kantipur), Tej Narsingh (Lalitpur), Ranajit (Bhaktapur).<\/li>\n<li>Works: Krishna Mandir–Siddhi Narsingh (1637); Rani Pokhari–Pratap Malla (1670); Nyatapola–Bhupatindra (1702).<\/li>\n<li>Araniko: White Stupa, Beijing.<\/li>\n<li>Khas kingdom: Nagaraj; Sinja (Jumla), winter capital Dullu (Dailekh); Ripu Malla at Lumbini 1312; Prithvi Malla's Dullu inscription 1357; Baise states.<\/li>\n<li>Karnat: Nanyadeva 1097; Simraungadh (Bara); Harisimhadeva fled 1324.<\/li>\n<li>Gorkha: Drabya Shah 1559; Ram Shah, justice.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Unification<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Prithvi Narayan Shah: born 27 Poush 1779 BS; Nuwakot 1744; Kirtipur 1766 (Kalu Pande killed 1757); Kantipur 25 September 1768 (Indra Jatra); Bhaktapur 1769; died 1 Magh 1831 BS at Devighat.<\/li>\n<li>Dibya Upadesh: yam between two boulders.<\/li>\n<li>Widest limits: Tista to Sutlej. Bhimsen Thapa, Mukhtiyar 1806–1837.<\/li>\n<li>Anglo-Nepal War 1814–1816: Nalapani–Balbhadra; Deuthal–Bhakti Thapa; Malaun–Amar Singh Thapa.<\/li>\n<li>Sugauli: signed 2 December 1815, ratified 4 March 1816; Mechi–Mahakali. Naya Muluk back in 1860.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Rana period (1846–1951)<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Kot Massacre 14 September 1846. Muluki Ain 1910 BS. Thapathali 1856.<\/li>\n<li>Order: Jung Bahadur, Ranodip, Bir, Dev, Chandra, Bhim, Juddha, Padma, Mohan.<\/li>\n<li>Dev: 114 days, Gorkhapatra (1958 BS). Chandra: Sati (1977), slavery (1981), Tri-Chandra (1975), treaty of 1923. Juddha: Nepal Bank (1994), four martyrs (1997). Padma: Act of 2004.<\/li>\n<li>First martyr Lakhan Thapa (1933 BS). Praja Parishad 1993 BS. Martyrs' Day Magh 16.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>2007–2017 BS<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>21 Kartik 2007 Indian Embassy; 7 Falgun 2007 democracy. M.P. Koirala 2008. United Nations 14 December 1955.<\/li>\n<li>Constitution 1 Falgun 2015; election from 7 Falgun 2015; 109 seats, Congress 74; B.P. Koirala Prime Minister 13 Jestha 2016.<\/li>\n<li>Takeover 1 Poush 2017, Article 55.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Panchayat (2017–2046 BS)<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Zones and districts 2018; Constitution 1 Poush 2019; Muluki Ain 2020; Land Reform 2021.<\/li>\n<li>Zone of Peace 2031 (116 countries). Referendum 20 Baisakh 2037: about 54.8% to 45.2% for reformed Panchayat.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>2046–2063 BS<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Movement I: 7 Falgun – 26 Chaitra 2046. Constitution 23 Kartik 2047. Elections 2048, 2051, 2056.<\/li>\n<li>Armed conflict from 1 Falgun 2052. Palace massacre 19 Jestha 2058. Royal takeover 19 Magh 2061.<\/li>\n<li>12-point understanding 7 Mangsir 2062. Movement II: 24 Chaitra 2062 – 11 Baisakh 2063. Peace accord 5 Mangsir 2063. Interim Constitution 1 Magh 2063.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Republic<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Election 28 Chaitra 2064; republic 15 Jestha 2065 (560–4); first President Ram Baran Yadav.<\/li>\n<li>Second election 4 Mangsir 2070. Constitution passed 30 Bhadra 2072 (507–25), proclaimed 3 Ashoj 2072; 35 Parts, 308 Articles, 9 Schedules.<\/li>\n<li>Elections 2074, 2079. Gen Z movement 23–24 Bhadra 2082; Sushila Karki interim Prime Minister 27 Bhadra 2082; election 21 Falgun 2082, Rastriya Swatantra Party 182 of 275; Balendra Shah Prime Minister 13 Chaitra 2082 🔄.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h114\">B. Top 20 facts<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>The <strong>Ashoka pillar at Lumbini (249 BC)<\/strong> carries the oldest inscription found in Nepal.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Changu Narayan inscription of Manadeva (464 AD)<\/strong> is the oldest dated inscription of the Kathmandu Valley.<\/li>\n<li>The first Kirat king was <strong>Yalambar<\/strong>; the last was <strong>Gasti<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Amshuverma<\/strong> built <strong>Kailashkut Bhawan<\/strong> and wrote <em><strong>Shabda Vidya<\/strong><\/em>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Nepal Sambat<\/strong> began in <strong>879 AD<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Jayasthiti Malla<\/strong> reformed society, weights and measures, and land grades.<\/li>\n<li>After <strong>Yaksha Malla<\/strong> died in <strong>1482<\/strong>, the Valley split into Kantipur, Lalitpur and Bhaktapur.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Khas kingdom<\/strong> had its capital at <strong>Sinja (Jumla)<\/strong>; the Sinja valley is regarded as the birthplace of the Nepali language.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Karnat dynasty<\/strong> ruled from <strong>Simraungadh (Bara)<\/strong> from 1097 to 1324.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Drabya Shah<\/strong> founded the Shah kingdom of Gorkha in <strong>1559 AD (1616 BS)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/strong> won <strong>Nuwakot in 1744<\/strong> and <strong>Kantipur on 25 September 1768<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Sugauli Treaty<\/strong> was signed on <strong>2 December 1815<\/strong> and ratified on <strong>4 March 1816<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Kot Massacre of 14 September 1846<\/strong> began 104 years of Rana rule.<\/li>\n<li><strong>Chandra Shamsher<\/strong> abolished <strong>Sati (1977 BS)<\/strong> and <strong>slavery (1981 BS)<\/strong> and opened <strong>Tri-Chandra College (1975 BS)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Nepal Praja Parishad (1993 BS)<\/strong> was the first political party; the <strong>four martyrs<\/strong> were executed in <strong>Magh 1997 BS<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Democracy was proclaimed on <strong>7 Falgun 2007 (18 February 1951)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>In the first general election (2015 BS) the <strong>Nepali Congress won 74 of 109 seats<\/strong>; <strong>B.P. Koirala<\/strong> became the first elected Prime Minister.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>referendum of 20 Baisakh 2037<\/strong> kept the Panchayat system by about <strong>54.8% to 45.2%<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>The <strong>Comprehensive Peace Accord<\/strong> was signed on <strong>5 Mangsir 2063 (21 November 2006)<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Nepal became a <strong>republic on 15 Jestha 2065<\/strong>, and the <strong>Constitution of Nepal<\/strong> was proclaimed on <strong>3 Ashoj 2072<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h115\">C. Top 25 MCQs<\/h3>\n<p>None of these repeats a question from the topics.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T1<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who was the last king of the Kirat dynasty?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Yalambar<\/li>\n<li>(b) Patuka<\/li>\n<li>(c) Gasti<\/li>\n<li>(d) Sthunko<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Gasti was the last Kirat king; Yalambar was the first.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T2<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The Ashoka pillar at Lumbini was rediscovered in 1896 by Dr Alois Führer and:<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Khadga Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(b) Chandra Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(c) Bir Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(d) Dev Shamsher<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Khadga Shamsher was then the governor of Palpa.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T3<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who built the palace called Kailashkut Bhawan?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Manadeva<\/li>\n<li>(b) Amshuverma<\/li>\n<li>(c) Narendradeva<\/li>\n<li>(d) Jayasthiti Malla<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Amshuverma ruled from Kailashkut Bhawan; Manadeva's palace was Managriha.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T4<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements about Nepal Sambat.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>It began in 879 AD.<\/li>\n<li>Tradition links it with Shankhadhar Sakhwa.<\/li>\n<li>It began in the reign of Manadeva.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Nepal Sambat began in the reign of Raghavadeva, about four centuries after Manadeva.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T5<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In which city did Araniko build the White Stupa?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Lhasa<\/li>\n<li>(b) Beijing<\/li>\n<li>(c) Kathmandu<\/li>\n<li>(d) Nanjing<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Araniko worked at the court of Kublai Khan and built the White Stupa in Beijing.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T6<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who built the five-storey Nyatapola temple of Bhaktapur?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Ranajit Malla<\/li>\n<li>(b) Yaksha Malla<\/li>\n<li>(c) Jitamitra Malla<\/li>\n<li>(d) Bhupatindra Malla<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Bhupatindra Malla built it in 1702.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T7<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the monument with the king linked with it.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Monument<\/th>\n<th>King<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. Rani Pokhari<\/td>\n<td>1. Jaya Prakash Malla<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. Taleju temple of Kathmandu<\/td>\n<td>2. Ranajit Malla<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. Golden Gate of Bhaktapur<\/td>\n<td>3. Pratap Malla<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. Kumari House<\/td>\n<td>4. Mahendra Malla<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-4, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-4, B-3, C-2, D-1<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-2, C-4, D-1<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Rani Pokhari–Pratap Malla, Taleju temple–Mahendra Malla, Golden Gate–Ranajit Malla, Kumari House–Jaya Prakash Malla.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T8<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who founded the Shah kingdom of Gorkha in 1559 AD?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Ram Shah<\/li>\n<li>(b) Nara Bhupal Shah<\/li>\n<li>(c) Drabya Shah<\/li>\n<li>(d) Prithvipati Shah<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Drabya Shah, a prince of Lamjung, became king of Gorkha in 1616 BS.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T9<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The saying &quot;If you do not get justice, go to Gorkha&quot; is linked with which king?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Ram Shah<\/li>\n<li>(b) Drabya Shah<\/li>\n<li>(c) Prithvi Narayan Shah<\/li>\n<li>(d) Rana Bahadur Shah<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Ram Shah was famous for his just rule and for fixing weights and measures.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T10<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In which battle was Kalu Pande killed?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Nuwakot<\/li>\n<li>(b) Kirtipur<\/li>\n<li>(c) Makwanpur<\/li>\n<li>(d) Sindhuligadhi<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — He fell in the first attack on Kirtipur in 1757.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T11<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Where did Prithvi Narayan Shah die?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Gorkha<\/li>\n<li>(b) Kathmandu<\/li>\n<li>(c) Bhaktapur<\/li>\n<li>(d) Devighat, Nuwakot<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — He died at Devighat on 1 Magh 1831 BS (11 January 1775).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T12<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the events in order of time, earliest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Treaty of Betrawati<\/li>\n<li>Kot Massacre<\/li>\n<li>Start of the Anglo-Nepal War<\/li>\n<li>Treaty of Thapathali<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 – 2 – 3 – 4<\/li>\n<li>(b) 3 – 1 – 2 – 4<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 – 3 – 2 – 4<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1 – 3 – 4 – 2<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — 1792, 1814, 1846, 1856.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T13<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who was the Mukhtiyar (chief minister) of Nepal during the Anglo-Nepal War?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Bhimsen Thapa<\/li>\n<li>(b) Mathbar Singh Thapa<\/li>\n<li>(c) Jung Bahadur<\/li>\n<li>(d) Kalu Pande<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Bhimsen Thapa held the post from 1806 to 1837.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T14<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>The first Muluki Ain (national legal code) of 1910 BS was issued in the time of which Prime Minister?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Bhimsen Thapa<\/li>\n<li>(b) Chandra Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(c) Jung Bahadur<\/li>\n<li>(d) Bir Shamsher<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Jung Bahadur had it prepared after his visit to Europe.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T15<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which Rana Prime Minister held office for only 114 days?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Bhim Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(b) Dev Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(c) Padma Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(d) Ranodip Singh<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Dev Shamsher ruled for 114 days in 1901 and was removed by Chandra Shamsher.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T16<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p><strong>Assertion (A):<\/strong> The British returned Banke, Bardiya, Kailali and Kanchanpur to Nepal in 1860.<\/p>\n<p><strong>Reason (R):<\/strong> Nepal had helped the British to put down the uprising of 1857 in India.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Both A and R are true, and R explains A.<\/li>\n<li>(b) Both A and R are true, but R does not explain A.<\/li>\n<li>(c) A is true, but R is false.<\/li>\n<li>(d) A is false, but R is true.<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — The land, called the Naya Muluk, was returned as a reward for Jung Bahadur's help.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T17<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who is honoured as the first martyr of Nepal?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Shukraraj Shastri<\/li>\n<li>(b) Dashrath Chand<\/li>\n<li>(c) Bhimdatta Panta<\/li>\n<li>(d) Lakhan Thapa Magar<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Lakhan Thapa Magar was hanged in 1933 BS (1877) for rising against Jung Bahadur.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T18<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>In which year was Tri-Chandra College founded?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1958 BS<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1968 BS<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1975 BS<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1994 BS<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — 1975 BS (1918), in the time of Chandra Shamsher.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T19<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Fill in the blank. The Government of Nepal Act, 2004 — the first constitution of Nepal — was announced by Prime Minister ______.<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Juddha Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(b) Mohan Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(c) Chandra Shamsher<\/li>\n<li>(d) Padma Shamsher<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — Padma Shamsher announced it on 13 Magh 2004 (26 January 1948).<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T20<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Which article of the Constitution of 2015 did King Mahendra use for the takeover of 1 Poush 2017?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Article 55<\/li>\n<li>(b) Article 127<\/li>\n<li>(c) Article 35<\/li>\n<li>(d) Article 115<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — Article 55 gave the King emergency powers.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T21<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Read the statements about the referendum.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>King Birendra announced it in 2036 BS.<\/li>\n<li>The reformed Panchayat side received about 54.8% of the votes.<\/li>\n<li>After it, the third amendment brought direct election of the Rastriya Panchayat by adult franchise.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<p>Which statements are correct?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 and 2 only<\/li>\n<li>(b) 2 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(c) 1 and 3 only<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1, 2 and 3<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — All three statements are correct.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T22<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Who chaired the interim council of ministers formed in Chaitra 2069 to hold the second Constituent Assembly election?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) Baburam Bhattarai<\/li>\n<li>(b) Khil Raj Regmi<\/li>\n<li>(c) Sushil Koirala<\/li>\n<li>(d) Ram Baran Yadav<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (b)<\/strong> — Khil Raj Regmi was Chief Justice at the time.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T23<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Match the election with the party that won the most seats.<\/p>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Election (BS)<\/th>\n<th>Largest party<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>A. 2015<\/td>\n<td>1. CPN (UML)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>B. 2051<\/td>\n<td>2. Rastriya Swatantra Party<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>C. 2064<\/td>\n<td>3. Nepali Congress<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>D. 2082<\/td>\n<td>4. Communist Party of Nepal (Maoist)<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) A-3, B-4, C-1, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(b) A-1, B-3, C-4, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(c) A-3, B-1, C-4, D-2<\/li>\n<li>(d) A-3, B-1, C-2, D-4<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (c)<\/strong> — Nepali Congress in 2015, CPN (UML) in 2051, CPN (Maoist) in 2064 and the Rastriya Swatantra Party in 2082.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T24<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Expected – Practice<\/span><\/p>\n<p>How many members voted in favour when the Constituent Assembly passed the Constitution of Nepal on 30 Bhadra 2072?<\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 601<\/li>\n<li>(b) 560<\/li>\n<li>(c) 532<\/li>\n<li>(d) 507<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (d)<\/strong> — 507 voted for and 25 against. The figure 560 belongs to the vote for the republic in 2065.<\/p>\n<p class=\"qid\"><strong>T25<\/strong> <span class=\"chip\">Pattern-Based – Reconstructed<\/span><\/p>\n<p>Arrange the institutions in the order in which they were founded, earliest first.<\/p>\n<ol>\n<li>Gorkhapatra<\/li>\n<li>Nepal Bank Limited<\/li>\n<li>Tri-Chandra College<\/li>\n<li>Tribhuvan University<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<ul>\n<li>(a) 1 – 3 – 2 – 4<\/li>\n<li>(b) 1 – 2 – 3 – 4<\/li>\n<li>(c) 3 – 1 – 2 – 4<\/li>\n<li>(d) 1 – 3 – 4 – 2<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p class=\"ans\"><strong>Answer: (a)<\/strong> — 1958, 1975, 1994 and 2016 BS.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h116\">D. Five short-answer questions<\/h3>\n<p><strong>1. Describe the role of Prithvi Narayan Shah in the unification of Nepal.<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> condition of the country — more than fifty small states · preparation: weapons, army, alliances, blockade of the Valley · steps: Nuwakot 1744, Makwanpur 1762, Kirtipur 1766, Kantipur and Lalitpur 1768, Bhaktapur 1769, eastern kingdoms 1773–1774 · defeat of Mir Qasim's and Kinloch's forces · capital moved to Kathmandu · Dibya Upadesh: foreign policy, economy, justice · assessment: founder of modern Nepal.<\/p>\n<p><strong>2. What were the main terms and effects of the Sugauli Treaty?<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> signed 2 December 1815, ratified 4 March 1816 · loss of land east of the Mechi, west of the Mahakali and in the Terai — about one third of the territory · British Resident in Kathmandu · limits on Nepal's dealings with other powers · Gorkha recruitment · later return of part of the Terai (1816) and the Naya Muluk (1860) · effects: end of expansion, fixed boundaries, inward-looking politics, boundary questions that continue today.<\/p>\n<p><strong>3. Give an account of the anti-Rana movements up to 2007 BS.<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> Lakhan Thapa (1933 BS) · Makai Parva (1977), Library Parva (1987), Prachanda Gorkha (1988) · Nepal Praja Parishad (1993) and the four martyrs (1997) · Biratnagar Jute Mill strike (2003) and Jayatu Sanskritam (2004) · Nepali National Congress (2003), Communist Party (2006), Nepali Congress (2006) · King Tribhuvan's move to the Indian Embassy; armed struggle; Delhi Agreement; 7 Falgun 2007.<\/p>\n<p><strong>4. Why did King Mahendra's takeover of 2017 BS take place, and what followed?<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> the King's wish for an active role · quarrels among and inside parties · opposition of landowners and former rulers to the reforms of the elected government · use of Article 55 on 1 Poush 2017 · arrest of leaders, ban on parties · Constitution of 2019 and the Panchayat system · reforms (Muluki Ain, land reform, zones and districts) alongside the loss of political freedom.<\/p>\n<p><strong>5. Explain the importance of the Comprehensive Peace Accord of 2063 BS.<\/strong>\n<em>Points for the answer:<\/em> signed 5 Mangsir 2063 by Girija Prasad Koirala and Prachanda · formal end of a ten-year war with about 17,000 dead · arms and armies placed under United Nations watch · Maoists brought into open politics · commitment to a Constituent Assembly election, human rights, and truth and reconciliation · base for the Interim Constitution, the republic and the Constitution of 2072 · unfinished work: justice for victims of the conflict.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h117\">E. Common mistakes<\/h3>\n<ol>\n<li>Giving the <strong>Changu Narayan inscription<\/strong> to Amshuverma instead of Manadeva.<\/li>\n<li>Calling <strong>Amshuverma<\/strong> a Lichhavi by birth, or placing <strong>Araniko<\/strong> in the Lichhavi period.<\/li>\n<li>Mixing the <strong>Malla kings of the Valley<\/strong> with the <strong>Khas &quot;Malla&quot; kings of Sinja<\/strong>.<\/li>\n<li>Placing <strong>Simraungadh<\/strong> in Dhanusha instead of Bara.<\/li>\n<li>Naming <strong>Kirtipur or Kathmandu<\/strong> as the first victory of Prithvi Narayan Shah; it was Nuwakot.<\/li>\n<li>Writing that the <strong>Sugauli Treaty<\/strong> returned the Naya Muluk; that happened in 1860.<\/li>\n<li>Counting <strong>Tanka Prasad Acharya<\/strong> among the four martyrs.<\/li>\n<li>Giving <strong>Gorkhapatra<\/strong> to Chandra Shamsher instead of Dev Shamsher.<\/li>\n<li>Confusing <strong>M.P. Koirala<\/strong> (first Prime Minister from the people) with <strong>B.P. Koirala<\/strong> (first elected Prime Minister).<\/li>\n<li>Mixing <strong>Falgun 7<\/strong> (Democracy Day), <strong>Baisakh 11<\/strong> (Loktantra Day) and <strong>Jestha 15<\/strong> (Republic Day).<\/li>\n<li>Saying the <strong>multi-party side<\/strong> won the referendum of 2037 BS.<\/li>\n<li>Mixing the <strong>12-point understanding<\/strong> (2062) with the <strong>Comprehensive Peace Accord<\/strong> (2063).<\/li>\n<li>Saying <strong>Bidya Devi Bhandari<\/strong> proclaimed the Constitution; it was Ram Baran Yadav.<\/li>\n<li>Mixing <strong>BS and AD<\/strong>: &quot;1947&quot;, &quot;1950&quot; and &quot;2007&quot; mean different things in each era.<\/li>\n<\/ol>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h118\">F. Status of the facts in this chapter<\/h3>\n<div class=\"tw\"><table>\n<thead>\n<tr>\n<th>Group of facts<\/th>\n<th>Status<\/th>\n<th>Basis<\/th>\n<\/tr>\n<\/thead>\n<tbody>\n<tr>\n<td>Gen Z movement of Bhadra 2082; interim government; election of 21 Falgun 2082 and its seat totals; Prime Minister since 13 Chaitra 2082<\/td>\n<td>Checked during this research 🔄<\/td>\n<td>Published election results and news reports (listed below). Not checked on the Election Commission's own site.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>All BS–AD date pairs from 1975 BS onward (about a hundred pairs)<\/td>\n<td>Checked with a calendar-conversion tool<\/td>\n<td>For each event, the date remembered in one calendar was converted and compared with the date remembered in the other; they agreed in every case used in this chapter<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dates before 1975 BS (unification, Sugauli Treaty, Kot Massacre, Gorkhapatra and others)<\/td>\n<td><strong>Standard textbook dates; not converted by tool<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>The tool does not cover these years. Mostly the year only is given.<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Dynasties, rulers, monuments, reforms, treaties, election seat totals up to 2079 BS, lists of &quot;firsts&quot;<\/td>\n<td><strong>Standard reference facts; not re-opened on official sites during this research<\/strong><\/td>\n<td>Cross-checked by arithmetic where possible: seats of 2015 BS add to 109; seats of 2082 BS add to 275<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Ancient period before the Lichhavis<\/td>\n<td>Tradition from chronicles<\/td>\n<td>Stated as tradition in the text<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<tr>\n<td>Number of Kirat kings; birth year of the Buddha; father of Bhrikuti; number of Rana prime ministers; deaths in the 2082 movement<\/td>\n<td>⚖️ Disputed<\/td>\n<td>Both versions are given in the text<\/td>\n<\/tr>\n<\/tbody>\n<\/table><\/div>\n<p><strong>Facts to re-check under UPDATE (🔄):<\/strong> the present Prime Minister, President and Speaker · seat totals of the 2082 election as finally published by the Election Commission · the official count of deaths in the 2082 movement · any amendment of the Constitution after 2077 BS · any change in the list of national heroes or public holidays linked with historical days.<\/p>\n<hr />\n<h3 id=\"h119\">G. References<\/h3>\n<p><strong>Official bodies to consult for the final word<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Department of Archaeology — inscriptions, monuments and excavations<\/li>\n<li>Lumbini Development Trust — Lumbini, Tilaurakot, Ramgram<\/li>\n<li>Nepal Law Commission — texts of the seven constitutions, the Muluki Ain, and the treaties in force<\/li>\n<li>Election Commission, Nepal — election dates and results<\/li>\n<li>Office of the President; Office of the Prime Minister and Council of Ministers — office-holders<\/li>\n<li>Ministry of Foreign Affairs — dates of diplomatic relations and treaties<\/li>\n<li>UNESCO World Heritage Centre — listing of Lumbini (1997) and the Kathmandu Valley (1979)<\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p><strong>Sources opened during this research<\/strong><\/p>\n<ul>\n<li>Election of 5 March 2026, summary of results: <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Nepalese_general_election\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2026_Nepalese_general_election<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Seat totals reported from the Election Commission: <a href=\"https://english.news.cn/20260313/1c1977a7e02d4f32964780643940421f/c.html\">https://english.news.cn/20260313/1c1977a7e02d4f32964780643940421f/c.html<\/a><\/li>\n<li>Gen Z movement, timeline: <a href=\"https://english.nepalnews.com/s/politics/one-year-of-the-gen-z-protests-the-complete-timeline-of-the-movement-and-its-aftermath/\">https://english.nepalnews.com/s/politics/one-year-of-the-gen-z-protests-the-complete-timeline-of-the-movement-and-its-aftermath/<\/a> and <a href=\"https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Nepalese_Gen_Z_protests\">https://en.wikipedia.org/wiki/2025_Nepalese_Gen_Z_protests<\/a><\/li>\n<\/ul>\n<p>The two encyclopaedia pages are not official sources. They were used only for recent events and were compared with news-agency reports.<\/p>\n<p><em>End of Chapter 2 (Part 6).<\/em><\/p>\n<p><strong>Next:<\/strong> Part 7 — Chapter 3: Constitution of Nepal. Type <strong>CONTINUE<\/strong>.<\/p>\n"});
